<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="no" ?>
<FinalDraft DocumentType="Script" Template="No" Version="5">
<Content>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>FADE IN:</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. A MOUNTAIN – DAWN</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>One white peak with its head in cloud, perfectly still. No music. No people. A silence that has been going on a very long time, and the distinct impression of being looked at.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Then, from nowhere, the drone of four heavy engines. The peak begins to soften at its edges, going paler and rounder as it climbs the sky.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Transition"><Text>MATCH DISSOLVE TO:</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. BAY OF BISCAY – NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A full moon, too bright. Black water below. A few hundred feet above it, alone in the sky: a B-17 FLYING FORTRESS, one engine trailing smoke, droning north. Spray streaks the nose. The pilot is flying low on purpose, and everyone aboard is trying not to think about why.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>SUPER: BAY OF BISCAY. SPRING, 1943.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. B-17 – WAIST – NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Cramped, rattling, freezing. Ammunition belts sag like bunting. The only light is a red bulb that makes everyone look like they&#x27;re already in trouble.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>SERGEANT PRIEST, thirties, Texan, works his gum like it owes him money. He is staring at the man across from him.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The man wears a fedora, pulled low, the brim bent by a decade of other people&#x27;s bad ideas. A bruised leather jacket over a borrowed flight suit. A coiled bullwhip on his hip, which has become a topic of discussion aboard this aircraft.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>INDIANA JONES, forty, eyes closed, does not look up.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PRIEST</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Doc, it&#x27;s twenty below. Why&#x27;s the hat still on?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(eyes closed)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Because it&#x27;s the only thing on this airplane I trust.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PRIEST</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>And the whip?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Don&#x27;t.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PRIEST</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Everybody&#x27;s asking about the whip.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It&#x27;s a long story.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PRIEST</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>We got eight hours.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It&#x27;s a longer story.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Beside him sits WERNER LINDT, fifties, thin, ink on his thumbs, a soft felt hat he refuses to remove and a leather satchel clutched to his stomach like a sleeping child. He is peeling an orange. It is the only orange for a thousand miles, and he knows it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>LINDT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You will have some.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He splits it. Puts the larger half in Indy&#x27;s hand.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I&#x27;m fine.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>LINDT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It was not a question. In Berlin there are no oranges. My wife says it is an indulgence to eat what is not in season in one&#x27;s own country.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Smart woman.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>LINDT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>She is in Hamburg. Or she is not.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He eats a segment with his eyes closed.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You&#x27;ll be in London by breakfast. Kippers. Tea. A very small egg.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>LINDT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I will be very happy with the egg.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy eats his half. It&#x27;s very good.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The engines change pitch.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. B-17 – COCKPIT – CONTINUOUS</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>CAPTAIN DELGADO, thick, tidy, Tulsa, stares through the windscreen. One shape rides the moon off the starboard wing: a JUNKERS 88, heavy with cannon, keeping pace. It isn&#x27;t attacking. It&#x27;s pacing them.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DELGADO</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(intercom)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Bandit, ten o&#x27;clock high. He&#x27;s just sitting there. I don&#x27;t like it. Gunners, light him up.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. B-17 – TAIL – NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>HOLLIS, twenty, a kid from Ohio, in the tail turret, opens up. Tracer fire tears across the dark and falls short of the Ju 88 by a mile.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. B-17 – WAIST – NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The whole airplane shudders with noise. Priest hammers at his gun. Indy is on his feet.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Something LANDS on the wing.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>It isn&#x27;t gunfire. A weight, settling where no weight should be. Indy feels it through the floor. He looks out the waist window.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A MAN is standing on the wing root.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Leather harness. Two steel canisters strapped to his back, trailing threads of fire. A few hundred feet over black water, two hundred miles an hour into the wind. Calm as a man on a train platform.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PRIEST</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That can&#x27;t be a man.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He keeps shooting anyway. His burst knocks the jumper off the wing. The man tumbles into the dark, canisters flaring, and hits the sea with a hiss.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(to himself)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>OSS bulletin. Test field near Stargard. Manned rockets.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PRIEST</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You read about these?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>None of the bulletins said they climb bombers like a ladder.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The waist hatch BANGS. A second jumper drops through boots-first, pistol already out, canisters hissing on his back.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy&#x27;s whip is in his hand before anyone sees him draw it. CRACK. It wraps the man&#x27;s gun arm. The pistol fires into the ceiling. Indy yanks; the man pitches forward, and his hand slaps down against his own harness.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>WHOOSH.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The canisters ignite inside the fuselage. The rocket man shoots the entire length of the airplane like a champagne cork, screaming, trailing fire, clips a hanging ammunition belt, and goes out the tail window in a spray of sparks.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Silence, except for the wind. Priest stares down the empty length of the plane.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PRIEST</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Doc.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I know.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A THIRD jumper hits the tail with a CLANG. Hollis&#x27;s guns stop.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOLLIS (V.O.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(muffled)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>There&#x27;s a guy on my turret! There&#x27;s a guy on my turret!</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PRIEST</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Shoot him!</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOLLIS (V.O.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He&#x27;s standing on my GUN!</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy is already running for the radio room.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. B-17 – RADIO ROOM – CONTINUOUS</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Lindt, the color of an eggshell, pressed against the bulkhead, satchel locked to his chest.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Stay down.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Lindt says something in German that doesn&#x27;t need translating.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The tail window EXPLODES inward. A FOURTH jumper swings through on a line, rifle shouldered. There is no time. One shot, flat and small in all that noise.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Lindt slides down the bulkhead. A dark hole in his chest nobody saw happen.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The jumper tears the satchel off Lindt&#x27;s shoulder. Indy hits him at a dead run. They crash through the radio table. The man breaks free, backs onto the sill, and slaps his harness.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>WHOOSH. He goes out the window like a cork, satchel strap in his fist.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy&#x27;s whip is already in the air. It catches the strap. The line goes taut and Indy leaves his feet, skates across the floor, fetches up against the window frame with his boots braced on the sill. Half a second to decide.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He takes it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. B-17 – TAIL – NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy slams belly-down onto the horizontal stabilizer, one hand clawing at frozen metal, the whip handle in the other. The slipstream tears the fedora off his head and pins it against the tail fin, where it flaps like a trapped bird.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Twenty feet away the rocket man hangs on twin flames, towing the satchel on its strap and Indy on the whip. Beyond him, the Ju 88 holds station, patient as a hearse.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A tug-of-war at two hundred miles an hour. Indy digs his boots into a seam in the aluminum. The strap sings. The satchel strains toward the Ju 88.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Come on, come on, come on—</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The strap TEARS.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The satchel streaks away into the dark. The rocket man catches the Ju 88&#x27;s wing and is hauled in like a fish. Indy is left holding the whip, and at the end of it a ragged triangle of leather torn from the satchel&#x27;s flap.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>In the Ju 88&#x27;s glass nose an OFFICER in a pale flying coat lifts two fingers to the peak of his cap. Polite. Almost friendly.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The Ju 88 banks away. The dark swallows it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy flicks the whip free of the leather. The lash snaps back, curls over the fin and plucks the fedora off it like a carny at a county fair. It drops into his hand.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He looks at it. Looks around for a witness. There is no one but the sea.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Nobody saw that.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He jams the hat on and starts crawling forward.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. B-17 – RADIO ROOM – NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy drops through the broken window, breathing hard, hat on crooked.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Lindt lies against the bulkhead. His half of the orange is on the floor beside him, one segment gone.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy kneels. Closes the man&#x27;s eyes. Takes off his own hat for exactly one second. Puts it back on.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INSERT – THE LEATHER FLAP</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Stitched inside it: a folded sheet. On one side, typed orders under an eagle and an oak-leaf wreath. On the other, a pencil sketch in Lindt&#x27;s own hand: a valley ringed with peaks, a tiny X. In the margin of the orders, in a different hand, one word, underlined twice:</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Shambhala.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DELGADO (V.O.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(intercom, thin)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Who&#x27;s alive back there?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(into the mic)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Not enough of us. Lindt&#x27;s gone.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DELGADO (V.O.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Hollis says he&#x27;d like it on the record he flare-gunned a man off his turret. And that Ju 88&#x27;s gone home. Four men dropped on my airplane, Doctor, and the other fellow just left. Whatever they came for, what did they get?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Half.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He looks at the page. Then at the orange.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Transition"><Text>SMASH CUT TO BLACK.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Silence. Then one low, brassy chord. White letters, like a stone coming out of the dark:</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDIANA JONES AND THE MOUNTAIN OF THE GODS</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Over black, the sound of a steam press. HISS. THUMP.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. MAJOR SHAW&#x27;S OFFICE – LONDON – MORNING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Two rooms above a tailor&#x27;s shop on Grosvenor Street. The blackout curtains are torn, so pale light falls across the desk in strips. The half-sheet lies flat under paperweights: a brass owl, a chipped inkwell, a stapler. The paper keeps trying to curl.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Downstairs, the tailor&#x27;s presser. HISS. THUMP. Every thump walks the brass owl a quarter inch toward the edge of the desk.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>MAJOR WALTER SHAW, fifties, gray with exhaustion, polishes his glasses on a handkerchief. Indy stands at the window, unshaven, still in the flight suit, hat back on, whip coiled at his hip like it never left.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SHAW</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Lindt&#x27;s dead. You&#x27;ve brought me half a sheet of paper.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Four men came out of the dark on rocket harnesses to get his bag. I got you what they missed.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SHAW</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Somebody wanted what he carried.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He turns the page so Indy can read it. The eagle. The oak-leaf wreath.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The Ahnenerbe.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SHAW</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The SS research outfit that does most of its studying with calipers, pointed at the skulls of the living. This is an operational order. Fuel, pack animals, cold-weather gear rated for altitude. Project shelved in thirty-nine, revived last autumn. A valley in Tibet, Jones. Three hundred miles from a railhead. Signed by an Obersturmbannführer Erich Kessler, doctor of anthropology.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>What&#x27;s in the valley?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SHAW</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The order says &quot;the find.&quot; Twice. In quotation marks both times.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy turns the page toward the light. The margin. That one word, underlined twice.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Shambhala.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SHAW</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Bless you.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>A professor in Vienna told me about it once. I didn&#x27;t like him. A lost homeland nobody can find on a map.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SHAW</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>And?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>They believe it. That&#x27;s worse than if they didn&#x27;t.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>THUMP. The owl reaches the edge. Without looking, Indy&#x27;s whip hand flicks; the lash snaps out, catches the owl mid-fall, and deposits it back on the desk. Shaw doesn&#x27;t look up. He hasn&#x27;t noticed since 1941.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SHAW</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>There&#x27;s an American mission going to Lhasa this spring. Real, above-board. Two men with the President&#x27;s letters and gifts for the boy on the throne. Nobody blinks at a third American joining as academic adviser. You&#x27;d fit the part without acting.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>And once I&#x27;m there?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SHAW</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You find out what the Ahnenerbe thinks it lost. And you make sure, by whatever means necessary, that it stays lost.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He pushes a folder across. A passport with Indy&#x27;s face. A letter from the State Department. A second letter on the stationery of a college in Connecticut.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SHAW (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Marshall&#x27;s dean has agreed to call it a sabbatical. He asked if you&#x27;d be back for spring term. I said I couldn&#x27;t speak to that.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>What did he say?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SHAW</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That the Egyptology section is a shambles and your lecture hall smells of gas. I took it to mean he likes you.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He doesn&#x27;t. He wants me to stop turning in grades late.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SHAW</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Then the cover is perfect.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Why me?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SHAW</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Donovan signed off personally. Asked for you by name. Said something about a mask in Mexico and a notebook in Havana, and a habit of getting things back from men who shouldn&#x27;t have them.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>When do I leave?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SHAW</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You already have.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>THUMP. The owl goes off the desk again. This time Indy lets it. It drops through a rotten floorboard. From the tailor&#x27;s shop below: a short, outraged cry.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SHAW (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Leave it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Transition"><Text>SMASH CUT TO:</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INSERT – A MAP OF THE WORLD</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A red line crawls across it to the beat of brisk, climbing engines. LONDON. GIBRALTAR. CAIRO. KARACHI. CALCUTTA. At each stop a rubber stamp comes down on a ticket in the corner of frame, and a hat brim sinks a little lower. The line reaches the top of India and keeps climbing, into the blank white place where the map gives up.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. THE HIMALAYA – DAY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Snow-capped peaks rise through broken cloud like teeth through gum. We are close. Too close.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>SUPER: THE HUMP. INDIA–TIBET FRONTIER.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. C-47 – COCKPIT – DAY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy in the jump seat, a down jacket that smells of someone else&#x27;s tobacco, the fedora held in both hands on his knee. The CO-PILOT makes small, urgent corrections.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>CO-PILOT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Lost eleven planes off this route last month. Weather, mostly. Sometimes the Japanese. Mostly weather.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Comforting.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>CO-PILOT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I didn&#x27;t say it to comfort you.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A wall of granite fills the windscreen. The pilot hauls the yoke. The plane claws up and over the ridge with fifty feet to spare.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy puts his hat back on, very slowly.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. NATU LA – ROPE BRIDGE – DAY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A cane-and-rope span over a gorge, swaying in the wind, planks missing like bad teeth. Two hundred feet of nothing below it, and a white river at the bottom having a better time than anyone up here.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>On the far side, a pack train of porters. Two PILGRIMS in rough robes sit by the anchor post, spinning prayer wheels, patient as stones.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy, sunburned, hollow-eyed, leads a gray gelding MULE. One white ear. Strong opinions about bridges.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The mule stops dead at the edge. Looks at the drop. Refuses.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>DAWA, fifteen, a Lepcha muleteer, calls out something to the porters across the gap. They howl with laughter.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>What did he say?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DAWA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He says the mule has more sense than the American.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy hands him the rope. Steps onto the bridge. The cane creaks like a ship&#x27;s timber.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Halfway across he notices three things. The porters have gone quiet. The pilgrims have stopped spinning their wheels. And under the hem of one robe, a boot. Polished. Nobody in the Himalaya polishes his boots.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(pleasantly)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Hello.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Both pilgrims stand. Two long knives come out of the robes. They go to work on the anchor ropes.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The left cable PARTS. The bridge lurches sideways. Planks slide out from under Indy&#x27;s boots. He doesn&#x27;t drop flat. He RUNS, uphill along a sagging bridge, straight at them.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The first pilgrim pulls a pistol from his sash. Indy is twenty feet away and closing. CRACK. The whip takes the pistol out of the man&#x27;s hand and sends it spinning into the gorge. The man stares at his empty fingers.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PILGRIM</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(in German, to himself)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Verdammt.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The second pilgrim hacks the last cable.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The bridge drops out from under Indy like a trapdoor. He leaps. The whip lashes around the anchor post; Indy swings out over the gorge, slams into the cliff face below the lip, and hangs there, one-handed, as the whole bridge falls away behind him. Planks tumble for a very long time.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Across the gap, on the near side, Dawa watches with his mouth open. The mule does not. The mule has seen this coming.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy looks up. Two pilgrims look down.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Boys.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A knife comes down. Indy catches the wrist and for one ugly moment the whole world is a man&#x27;s teeth and a drop. He kicks the man&#x27;s ankle out. The first pilgrim goes over the edge with a rope still tangled around his boot, hauled down by the tail of the fallen bridge.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The second brings up his knife. Indy&#x27;s boot meets the middle of his robe. The man staggers back onto the lip of the cliff. The whip finds his ankle. A tug.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He&#x27;s gone. The gorge doesn&#x27;t even echo.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy hauls himself over the edge and lies on his back with his hat over his face. The porters stare at him from behind a boulder. Nobody says anything.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Twenty minutes later. Half a mile upstream, where the gorge opens out and the river spreads thin over flat stones, Dawa leads the mule across a ford. The mule walks through the water with its eyes open, unhurried.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(from under the hat)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>There&#x27;s a ford.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DAWA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>There is always a ford.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Why didn&#x27;t anyone mention it?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DAWA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The mule did.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The mule arrives. Shakes itself like a dog, soaking Indy.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DAWA (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I did say. The mule has more sense.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. GANGTOK BAZAAR – DAY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A crowded market, prayer flags overhead. A tailor with a cast in one eye measures Indy twice. A cobbler resoles his boots in yak hide. A woman at a stall presses a twist of dried cheese into his hand and mimes: suck it, don&#x27;t chew it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy pockets it, bewildered.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>At a trinket stall, a MERCHANT lifts a shriveled brown thing on a stand with great ceremony.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MERCHANT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Yeti scalp, sahib. Very old.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That&#x27;s a goat.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MERCHANT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Sahib is collector?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Sahib is expert.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He sets the &quot;scalp&quot; down. Behind him, half hidden by a stack of prayer wheels, a figure in a fur-lined cap, copper hair tucked beneath it, watches. A camera strap. A glint of glass.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The figure ducks out of sight as Indy turns. Prayer wheels spin. Nothing there.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. THE RESIDENCY LAWN – GANGTOK – DAY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A lawn at the edge of the world. A thousand feet of air beyond it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>GERALD WHITCOMBE, His Majesty&#x27;s Political Officer for Sikkim, narrow, sunburned, in shirtsleeves, tees up a golf ball.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>WHITCOMBE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Only course in Sikkim. Par three. A thousand feet of air, and the hazard is a river.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He swings. The ball sails out over the gorge and is gone.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>WHITCOMBE (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Shaw&#x27;s cable said you&#x27;d come through eventually. He didn&#x27;t say on foot.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I had help. Two pilgrims with very good boots tried to push me off a mountain.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Whitcombe&#x27;s second swing goes into a hedge.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>WHITCOMBE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Germans?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Dead ones, now.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>WHITCOMBE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Ah. Then they have people this side of the border, which is more than I was told.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He presses a club into Indy&#x27;s hands like a man recruiting.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>WHITCOMBE (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Here&#x27;s what we know, and I&#x27;d rather not say how.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Wireless intercept.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>WHITCOMBE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Which is why London can&#x27;t simply protest to Lhasa. The moment the Kashag asks how we know, we&#x27;ve told Berlin which of its codes we read. Hence a professor instead of a démarche.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy swings. The ball veers off at an angle that suggests it has somewhere to be.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>WHITCOMBE (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>A German party crossed in last autumn, calling itself a trade delegation, which fooled nobody, since trade delegations don&#x27;t carry theodolites and crates stenciled with a Reich eagle. They&#x27;re in a valley three days east of Lhasa. The Tibetans call it Shomo. The quiet place. Run by Obersturmbannführer Erich Kessler, who was in that same valley in thirty-nine and has apparently spent four years wanting to go back.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The page had a word in the margin. Shambhala.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Whitcombe lowers the club. His face goes still.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>WHITCOMBE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That word gets men killed up here, Dr. Jones. And not always by other men.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He retrieves his bag, which means the round is over.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>WHITCOMBE (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>What I can tell you is what we&#x27;ve seen. Caravans going in, heavier each time. Crates two porters can&#x27;t lift. And six weeks ago a Tibetan trader who runs that route came through Gangtok white as a sheet. Wouldn&#x27;t say why. Hasn&#x27;t been back.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I need a guide.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>WHITCOMBE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>There&#x27;s a trading post three days east called Rateng. Ask for Tsering Wangmo. She runs pack trains for the wool traders and knows every goat track to the Chinese border. Her brother went in with the Germans in thirty-nine as a guide and didn&#x27;t come out.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He hands Indy a sealed envelope.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>WHITCOMBE (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>A letter of introduction. It will do you no good whatever. She regards the Political Officer as a sort of weather.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Then why give it to me?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>WHITCOMBE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>So that if you don&#x27;t come back, someone can say whom to ask.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He tees up the last ball.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>WHITCOMBE (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(rather gently, to the valley)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Fore.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He hits it into the clouds.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. THE OLD MAN PASS – A LEDGE ABOVE A RIVER – DAY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Dawn. Mist in the gorges. The road out of Gangtok toward Rateng climbs into thinner air.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A ledge two mules wide, cut into a mountainside that goes up on the left until it stops being rock and becomes weather, and drops on the right to a river like a dropped thread.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy rides the gray gelding. Dawa leads, with two porters and a short train of pack animals behind.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DAWA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Two men behind us. Since Gangtok.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He doesn&#x27;t turn his head.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DAWA (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Their ponies are good. Their coats are good. Their boots are very good.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy has been watching those boots for a day and a half. Nobody in the Himalaya polishes his boots.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Half a mile back, two RIDERS in sheepskin coats keep pace with the patience of men who&#x27;ve been told to wait for a better place.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The road gives them one.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Ahead, the shelf narrows to a church pew and crosses a scar of old rockfall. Above it, wedged in the cliff like a bad tooth, hangs a boulder the size of a bullock cart, knotted with prayer flags.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DAWA (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That is the Old Man. Every spring the mountain tries to take him. Every spring the lamas climb up and tie a new flag. He has not fallen in ninety years.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Does he know that?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DAWA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He is a rock.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>BEHIND THEM, a pistol CRACKS.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The bullet hits the cliff a yard ahead of the gelding&#x27;s nose. Dawa hits the dirt. The porters drop their loads and sprint for the bend. And the gelding, who has stopped exactly once for a reason in two hundred miles, stops again, all four feet planted.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy rolls off on the cliff side and puts the mule between himself and the road.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Good mule.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The mule does not care. It is eight hundred pounds of firm opinion. For the first time since the cane bridge, Indy is grateful for it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Under the gelding&#x27;s belly: two riders, forty yards off, coming at a walk like men with all the time in the world.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy has six rounds and a revolver that throws left. He looks at the riders. At the narrow place on the shelf. At the Old Man.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Tell me when the first pony is under the flags.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DAWA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(lifting his face from the dirt)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You want to shoot the mountain.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I want to ask it a favor.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He fires. A prayer flag tears off the line. He fires again, chipping the Old Man above a dark seam where meltwater has been working at the stone for a hundred years.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Nothing happens.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The lead rider LAUGHS, one short bark, and comes on.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DAWA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Perhaps he does not like you.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Tell me when.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DAWA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Now.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy puts the third round into the seam. The fourth.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A long groan, like a ship settling.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The Old Man LEANS, politely, as if asked to move down a pew. Then the whole hillside goes.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A slab the size of a house front pours across the shelf with a roar like a freight yard. Pony, Mauser, man: gone in a wall of brown dust. Indy drags Dawa flat under the cliff as a rock the size of a bread oven goes over their heads, close enough to part his hair, and out into nothing.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The dust thins. Forty yards of shelf are gone. In their place lies raw rubble, and across it, knee-deep, holding his rifle over his head like a man fording a river, comes the SECOND RIDER.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>No pony. No cap. Not a word. He picks his way from stone to stone with terrible manners.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy fires once. The dust has done something to his aim. The man swings the rifle down, fires from the hip, and shoots the heel off Indy&#x27;s boot.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Rope! The lead rope. Give me the end!</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He knots it round the gelding&#x27;s pack frame. The gelding does not twitch.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Stay behind the mule.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DAWA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Behind the mule?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Everyone should.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He walks out toward the rider with both hands wide and empty, like a man about to surrender.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Behind him, Dawa, who has been tying things to mules since he was six, flicks a loop of rope along the ground.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The rider takes one more step to be sure. His boot goes into the loop.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The other end is tied to the one object in the Himalaya that will not move.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The rider goes down on his face, rifle skating away. Indy is on him before he stops bouncing.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>It&#x27;s not a clean fight. The rubble slides under them like a conveyor belt. The man is lean, fast, trained by someone good. Twice Indy finds a knife where he didn&#x27;t expect one. But the loop holds, and the mountain is still moving, and the last foot of shelf, which has been holding on out of habit, quits.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>They go over together.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy&#x27;s hand finds a root. His other hand finds the man&#x27;s wrist.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>For a second he has him. The rider hangs above a thousand feet of river and looks up at Indy with perfect calm. Indy understands that he has already decided what kind of prisoner he will be.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Don&#x27;t.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The man opens his hand.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy watches him fall until the spray swallows him.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Quiet. The porters creep back round the bend, not meeting anyone&#x27;s eye. The gelding steps calmly forward onto the new rubble and begins to crop a tuft of grass growing out of the wreckage, as if the whole affair had been somebody else&#x27;s idea.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy hauls himself up. Looks at his missing boot heel. At the mule.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You could have helped.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The mule chews.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. RATENG – TRADING POST – DAY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Less a town than an idea of one. Stone houses with barley drying on the roofs. A monastery painted the color of old blood. A market square where yak-hair tents trade in salt, wool and rumor in roughly equal measure.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy limps through it, one boot heel missing, asking for a name. Three traders give him three directions: the universal response of people who&#x27;ve decided a stranger&#x27;s business isn&#x27;t theirs to speed along.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A fourth, an OLD TRADER missing most of his teeth, looks at Indy&#x27;s boots. At his hands.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>OLD TRADER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You&#x27;re not here for wool.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>No.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>OLD TRADER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Then you&#x27;re here about the valley.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He spits, with ceremony, onto the frozen ground.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>OLD TRADER (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Her tent is at the east edge, past the prayer wheels. One thing. She does not like to be thanked. If you thank her, she will charge you. I thanked her once, for mending a harness. Two rupees. I have not thanked anyone since.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Understood.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. TSERING&#x27;S TENT – RATENG – CONTINUOUS</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Black yak-hair stretched over lashed poles. Beside it, a small cairn of the kind built for the dead when there is no body to bury.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>TSERING WANGMO, early thirties, a head shorter than Indy and has not noticed, crouches at a cookfire resetting a packsaddle buckle with hands that have done it ten thousand times. She looks up with the flat appraisal of a woman who decided before he opened his mouth that whatever he wants is going to cost her.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You&#x27;re the American. The trader with no teeth sent a boy ahead.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>News travels fast.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>News is the only thing that does. You want to go into Shomo.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I need a guide.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I know a great many countries. I don&#x27;t go into that one.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She goes back to the saddle. The conversation is over. Except she hasn&#x27;t told him to leave.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The Germans there now are the ones who went in four years ago. I was told your brother guided that expedition.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Her hands stop.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I was told he didn&#x27;t come back.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You were told a great deal for a man who&#x27;s been in Rateng half an hour.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She stands.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Why do you want into that valley? The truth. Not the version you&#x27;d give a magistrate.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>A month ago four of their men attacked my plane using rocket harnesses strapped to their backs. To get at a defector. The defector&#x27;s papers say they&#x27;re going back to Shomo to finish something they started in thirty-nine. I don&#x27;t know what. I know my government is scared enough of the answer to fly me around half the world.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That is honest. It is not why you came to my tent instead of someone else&#x27;s.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I was told you hate that valley as much as I&#x27;m going to.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Something crosses her face. Quick, controlled. A wound that scarred over wrong and still pulls when the weather changes.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Hold this.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>That is as close to an invitation as he&#x27;s going to get.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>&quot;This&quot; is a BLACK MARE&#x27;s cracked left hind hoof. Tsering bends the leg up and puts the whole thing in Indy&#x27;s hands. The mare leans her full weight on him to see what will happen. Tsering takes a rasp from her belt and goes to work.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She passes him a bowl of salted, buttered tea without apology. He drinks it one-handed with a horse leaning on him, because refusing would cost more than the taste.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Do you pray?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Not often.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Do you believe in what the Germans are looking for?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I believe in what people will do for it. I watched a man cross a desert on his knees for a piece of wood. It was only wood. I never told him.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The rasp stops. The mare shifts another hundred pounds onto him.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That is a bad answer. It is the answer of a man who has been disappointed.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She says it without unkindness, the way she might say a horse favors the near side.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I would rather you believed in something. A man who believes in nothing runs the wrong way when the shooting starts.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I believe in my notebook.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Then keep it dry.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She refills the bowl. The mare shifts. The new tea goes down Indy&#x27;s leg.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Like that.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Beside the tent, a big YAK-CROSS has been eating Indy&#x27;s fedora since he sat down. It has reached the crown. Indy notices at the word &quot;calipers.&quot;</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Don&#x27;t pull. She will swallow it out of spite.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He lets go. The hat goes in by inches. Neither of them mentions it again.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Tsering drives a nail into the mare&#x27;s new shoe at the end of each sentence, so her brother&#x27;s whole life arrives in short hard strokes.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Norbu. The best high-altitude man in three valleys. Better than our father, who taught him. The Germans hired him in thirty-eight for more money than our family had seen in a decade. At first he wrote proud letters about the young scientist in charge. A serious one, who measured people&#x27;s heads with brass calipers.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Nail.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Then he stopped writing about the measuring. He wrote about a place high in Shomo that the herders wouldn&#x27;t graze near for double the fees.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Nail.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The official story is that he died of altitude sickness on the descent, four days before the expedition left. My brother had walked those heights since he was twelve. He did not die of altitude sickness. In four years nobody in that expedition has explained it to me. I have asked in more than one language.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The fire pops.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>And now the same man is going back.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>With more crates and more men and more rocket harnesses than the first time. And an American who says his own government is afraid of the answer has walked into my tent and drunk my tea.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She looks at him. Nothing soft in it. But nothing closed either, which is as far open as Tsering&#x27;s face goes.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I will take you to Shomo, Dr. Jones. Not for your government. Not for mine. I am going to stand in that valley and learn what my brother saw that was worth killing him to keep quiet.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She drives the last nail and sets the hoof down. The mare, released, immediately stands on Indy&#x27;s foot.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Agreed.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I haven&#x27;t finished. The whole truth, every time. Whatever you learn about my brother, you tell me. Even the parts you think will spare me. I have had four years of people deciding what I could bear to hear. I am finished with that kind of kindness.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Agreed.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>And he means it more the second time. He made that exact promise once on a different dock to a different woman, and very nearly didn&#x27;t keep it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Forty rupees a day. The feed for the animals is yours.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy counts what&#x27;s left of Shaw&#x27;s money.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I have thirty-eight.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Then we go for one day less than planned.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That isn&#x27;t how it works.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It is how it works in my tent. By the second day you will owe me more than you have. A man pays better when he is in debt.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Is that a general principle?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It is arithmetic.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She drops his pack on a scale made of two baskets and a pole and begins unloading it onto a carpet. The sulfa powder she approves. The salt tablets she sniffs and puts aside. A Sanskrit grammar she holds up between two fingers like a dead mouse.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It weighs one kilo. At fifteen thousand feet one kilo is two.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>If I find anything written down there, I&#x27;ll need it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>If you find anything written down there, you will need to be alive.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She opens it to the middle, tears out forty pages, hands them over folded.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>These. The rest stays. I will keep it for you.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That&#x27;s not how you treat a book.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It is how I treat a mule. I do not carry what it does not need.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She lifts the bullwhip out of the pack by its handle and lets it uncoil down her arm like something that might still be alive.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>And this.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That stays.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It is a rope that holds nothing.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It holds more than you&#x27;d think.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She considers him. Flicks her wrist at a tent peg six feet away. The lash takes the peg out of the ground and lays it in her open palm. She looks at it, unimpressed, and hands the whip back.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It is a rope with opinions. Keep it. It weighs less than your book.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She counts his socks next, laying them in a row like dead fish. Frowns.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Three.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I had four.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Then someone is walking behind you wearing one.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She says it without looking up, as a remark about mule feed, and goes on unloading. Indy, tired and cold, takes it for a joke.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>On the far side of the market, among the tents, a camera lens catches the sun for half a second. Then it&#x27;s gone.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Tsering walks him behind the tent to the pack animals tied in a line. The black mare, whose hoof he now knows personally, is vain. The big yak-cross is lazy. The little GRAY MULE at the end will carry anything and bite anyone who stands behind it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>This one is yours.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The biting one.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The honest one.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She holds the lamp to his boots. The yak-hide soles from Gangtok are already splitting. She goes into the tent and comes back with a pair of felt-lined boots that belonged to someone larger than her brother and smaller than Indy.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Try.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>They&#x27;re a size too small. He says they fit.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You are lying. Good. It means you will complain less.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>What do I owe you?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Nothing. They were not mine to sell.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She doesn&#x27;t say whose they were, and Indy has the sense not to ask. He walks back toward the fire in boots that pinch, past a cairn he decides not to look at.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>We leave at first light. Four days to the valley floor. Get what sleep you can, American. It will be the last easy sleep either of us has for some time.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. HIGH PASS – DAY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Sixteen thousand feet. Wind like paper tearing. A pack train of nine animals crawls up a ridge in single file.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy, ghost-white, stops every few hundred yards, hands on his knees. Every time he does, Tsering stops too, three paces ahead, and waits with her back to him.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>At the top stands a cairn the height of a man, hung with white prayer flags. She walks around it once, clockwise, touching a stone. Indy, who has been to enough ruins to know when not to joke, waits.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You are slow.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I&#x27;m aware.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The Germans will not walk this pass. There is a road on the other side, and Germans like roads. Four days. Not five. If you faint, I leave you at the next cairn.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That&#x27;s the most comforting thing anyone&#x27;s said to me this month.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I was not trying to comfort you.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She starts down.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. THE SNOWFIELD – DAY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Below the pass the trail drops onto a snowfield the size of a parade ground. Indy&#x27;s boots crunch. Under each step the snow answers with a hollow, drumlike note, as though the mountain has been built with a room in it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Tsering stops the train with one raised hand and tilts her head, like a woman listening to a stranger in the next room.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>One at a time. Quietly. If anything speaks, it will not be to you.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The snow?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Everything.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>They&#x27;re a hundred and fifty yards across when the sky begins to HUM.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A low three-note chord, coming up from the north. Indy feels it in his teeth. Over the ridge, low enough that he can see the corrugations on its skin, comes a JUNKERS 52, painted the dull gray of old pewter, droning toward the valley with something heavy in its belly.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(very quietly)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The iron bird. It comes every ten days. Dr. Jones. Do you hear the snow?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He does. It sounds like a crowd drawing breath.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A single flat CRACK, like a rifle shot in a cathedral. A bright line shoots across the slope a hundred yards above them, thin as a pencil mark.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>For one full second an acre of snow sits there, thinking about it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Then it sighs, and begins to slide.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Cut the rope!</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Her knife is out. The lead line parts.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Across! Not down, across!</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She slaps the nearest mule on the rump. Nine animals who have been told all their lives not to bolt discover they&#x27;ve been given permission. They scatter along the slope in a spray of bells, and the slope comes after them with a sound like a train going into a tunnel.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy runs. The snow comes up to his knees like wet cement. The roar is behind him. Beside him.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A hand closes on his collar and hauls him onto something hard and curved that smells powerfully of yak: a bowed, heavy PACKSADDLE of lashed wood and hide, shaped, if you&#x27;re generous and in a hurry, like a tin tray.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Sit.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>On what?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>On that.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She drops onto it behind him, arms locked round his waist, boots braced.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Lean when I lean. Do not fight it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>How do I steer?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You do not steer.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>What do I do?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You pray.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>To what?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Whatever you like. The mountain is not particular.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The avalanche reaches them. The world goes white and fast.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. THE AVALANCHE – CONTINUOUS</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>It isn&#x27;t a fall. It&#x27;s a ride.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The saddle skates out onto the crest of the slide with the whole slope roaring underneath. Tsering leans left; Indy leans left. A BOULDER the size of a car comes out of the white dead ahead.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Right!</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She says it in exactly the tone she uses for pack lists. They round the boulder so close he feels the cold come off the rock.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You said I don&#x27;t steer!</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You do not! That was leaning!</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>What&#x27;s the difference?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Steering is what you were doing before I told you not to!</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The slope ends in a lip of black rock with nothing beyond it but air.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Tsering—</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Now!</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She throws her weight forward. The saddle kicks off the lip like a ski jump.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>They sail out over a hundred feet of empty space in a spray of snow and splintered wood, and for one long moment Indiana Jones hangs over the Himalaya on a yak&#x27;s packsaddle with an extremely calm Tibetan woman and thinks, with perfect clarity, that nobody at Marshall College is ever going to believe a word of this.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>They land in the soft wreck of the runout, bounce twice, and come to rest in a drift. Silence so thick it rings. The saddle is in a dozen pieces.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(from somewhere under his left arm)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Count.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Count what?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Your fingers.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Ten.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Then you may count the mules.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. GRAVEL BAR BELOW THE SLOPE – LATER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The mules stand a quarter mile down, shaking snow off their coats in disgust. Tsering counts twice. Her shoulders come down an inch.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The honest gray stands apart on a boulder, looking back up the mountain with the smug expression of an animal proved right about everything.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>High above, the Junkers drones on over the ridge, wingtips glinting, its crew admiring the scenery.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>They didn&#x27;t even know we were there. I think that&#x27;s worse.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It is how the mountain is, too.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She shakes snow from her hair.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Nothing in these valleys has ever set out to kill anyone. It is only ever in the way.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Then I&#x27;ll make a point of staying out of its way.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>No. You will not.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She goes to count the mules again.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. THE BOUNDARY STONES – DAY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A shelf of black rock below the last pass, where the trail runs between two rows of standing slabs, each the height of a man, leaning like old teeth. Lichen has eaten their faces. No cairn. No prayer flags. Even the mules slow down.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Tsering stops the train. She does not step between the stones. She walks around them.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Problem?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Herders go around. Always. Nobody remembers why. That is how you know it is old.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy has already stepped between them. Of course he has. He crouches at the nearest slab and wipes the lichen away with his sleeve.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Carvings. A line of tall figures, long in the arm, hands raised, palms out. Under them, a row of strokes cut in groups of five: four short, one long across. Row under row, going back until the stone gives out.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Pre-Buddhist. Bön, maybe older. Nobody carved a prayer flag on this. No gods. No lamas. Just people, standing there with their hands open.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>A warning.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Everybody says warning. Look at the hands. A warning holds up one palm. This is both. It isn&#x27;t stop. It&#x27;s we&#x27;re here.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He traces the line of strokes.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>A border marker. Somebody saying, this is where we live, and we&#x27;d like you to knock. But why count a border?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>To know when someone crosses.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Neither of them says anything for a while. The wind moves through the gap between the slabs and makes a sound very close to a word.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy takes a page from the forty she left him, lays it across the carving, and rubs it with the flat of his pencil until the figures come up white out of the gray. He folds it into his jacket. Then he stands, looks at the stones, looks at the woman who walked around them with a thousand years of herders in her feet, and steps out of the row the long way, around the last slab.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You walked through.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I&#x27;m walking out.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That is not how it works.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Is that a general principle?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It is manners.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. RIDGE ABOVE SHOMO – DAY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The valley opens below them: gray, treeless, immense. At its far end a column of woodsmoke rises thin and steady into air too cold to hold it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy has gone pale and slow. Altitude has been quietly eating his thinking for days. Tsering sits him on a rock and looks him over like a woman choosing a horse.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>What is your mother&#x27;s name?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Why?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Answer.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Eleanor.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>It arrives half a second late, like a stranger stepping off the wrong train.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>How many mules do I have?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Eleven. Twelve, with mine.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Nine. Two stayed at the last pass. You watched me tie them.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She waits.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That is the mountain, not your mind. Count again, and count what is there.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He counts. Nine, standing in a line on the shale, one of them biting. It takes most of a minute.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Good. Most men count what they expect.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Behind the camp, at the valley&#x27;s far end, a peak stands apart from the others. A white tooth with its head in cloud. The ground rises toward it in a long clean ramp, as if the whole valley were built to approach it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>What&#x27;s that one called?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Lhari. The mountain of the gods. Every ridge in Tibet is called that by somebody.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She doesn&#x27;t look at it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The herders here do not say it aloud. It is not a name. It is an address.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She pulls him down behind a shoulder of granite.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>We watch today. If we go down now with no plan beyond wanting to see, we end up where my brother ended up.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>They lie on their stomachs. Field glasses go back and forth.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Below: six large tents. A generator shed with a stovepipe. A mule line. Twenty-two men, a corporal who patrols whistling the same eight bars, and three civilians in good coats who never carry rifles and never stay outdoors more than a few minutes.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Scientists.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>They walk like men who have been told they will not need to be here long.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>And roped off by itself, with fence posts and wire: a second enclosure that holds nothing Indy can see moving.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That fence wasn&#x27;t in Lindt&#x27;s drawings. A survey camp doesn&#x27;t fence off empty ground unless there&#x27;s something in it they don&#x27;t want getting out.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>At noon, the WIND STOPS.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Not a lull. It ceases, all at once. The silence is so total Indy hears the blood in his ears. Down in the camp, the German flags go limp.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(murmuring)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The herders say the wind stops here every noon for as long as it takes to say one prayer. They say the mountain is listening. Do not speak until it passes.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Ninety seconds. A mule shifts. A man coughs half a mile away. A tin cup is set down on a table.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Then the wind comes back with a long dry rush, and the valley goes back to being merely a place where men work.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. RIDGE ABOVE SHOMO – DUSK</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A bell rings. The whole camp goes to dinner at once. Only two GUARDS stay out, standing by the pen. One takes off his gloves to light a cigarette, puts them back on, and never once looks at the fence behind him.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>It is the first thing all day that frightens Indy.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>A man doesn&#x27;t turn his back on a lion cage for no reason. He does it when he&#x27;s been told the lion is asleep.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. SCREE SLOPE ABOVE THE PEN – NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>They crawl the last mile on their stomachs through scree that moves under them with every yard. The generator labors, muffled by snow. The fenced enclosure resolves into a pen thirty feet square, dug into the slope and shored with timber by someone who expects the walls to be tested.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Something inside it MOVES.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy goes still, the way he&#x27;s learned to around animals that haven&#x27;t decided whether to notice him.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A shape rises from the far timber. In the thin moonlight it stands taller than a man, considerably taller, shoulders too wide for the rest of it. One arm reaches up and closes around a fence timber, testing it. Feeling for give.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(barely audible)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That is not an animal.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>No.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>My brother&#x27;s letters. The place the herders wouldn&#x27;t graze.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Her voice has gone thin. Not fear exactly. More a woman finding out that a thing she has spent four years imagining is true.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He called it migoi. Wild man. I thought it was a story to keep children off dangerous ground.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The shape lets go of the timber. Draws something hard along the post, once. Sits back against the shoring.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>For a moment, before it folds its arms across its knees, its face turns toward the dark where they lie. And something about the weight behind the eyes is close enough to a man looking at other men that the hair stands up on Indy&#x27;s arms in a way four days of cold haven&#x27;t managed.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A light comes on in the nearest tent. VOICES. A door flap scrapes.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Move.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. OUTCROP ABOVE THE CAMP – LATER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A candle stub, burning too low to carry light. They&#x27;ve put a mile of broken ground behind them.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Around two in the morning Indy notices Tsering is shaking, steadily, and has been long enough to stop noticing. He takes off his sheepskin coat and puts it over her shoulders without a word. She lets him.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You will freeze.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I&#x27;ve been colder. A mountain in Utah. A boy of fourteen, and a train.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He turns up his collar.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It&#x27;s a long story and I come off badly.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Tell it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Another time.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>There may not be another time.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>But she doesn&#x27;t push. He hears her breathing slow.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(much later, to the candle)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>They found it in thirty-nine. Or they found proof of it. And Norbu knew. And four days before they left, my brother had an accident. I think Kessler has spent four years of a war wanting to come back and finish catching what my brother helped him find.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Then I need to know what Kessler thinks it is. I need his field reports.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He pinches out the candle.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Every camp this size runs on local labor. Porters, cooks, men who carry water and don&#x27;t ask questions. Somewhere down there is somebody who&#x27;s had four years to start asking anyway.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>And if there isn&#x27;t?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Then we find another way in. I didn&#x27;t fly halfway around a war to turn back at a fence.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. SCREE SLOPE – DUSK – NEXT DAY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy and Tsering lie flat behind a boulder, watching the camp. A slight figure in a quilted coat gone thin at the elbows comes up through the scree with both hands raised and empty, moving like someone who has rehearsed the approach.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Tsering has her knife out before Indy has his revolver. The man stops at twenty feet and says something in Tibetan, fast and low. Her shoulders come down an inch.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PEMBA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Pemba. I carry for the Germans. Water, wood, the heavy boxes. Four years, on and off.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Since before the war got to this part of the world.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PEMBA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Since before the pen.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>That one word does more than a paragraph. He crouches on his heels and looks at Tsering longer than at Indy.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PEMBA (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You are Norbu&#x27;s sister. I was there in thirty-nine. Not close to him at the end, but in the camp, the week it happened. I thought every year that someone should tell his family what the camp believed, even if it was a lie built to cover another lie.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Tell me.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Her voice has none of its usual flatness. Thin and urgent, a rope pulled tighter than it was built for.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PEMBA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Not here.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He glances down the slope and goes flat. A PATROL TORCH is swinging across the scree below. Indy and Tsering are on their faces beside him before they&#x27;ve decided to be. Pemba finishes the rest of his plan into the dirt, in a whisper, while the beam crosses six feet over their heads.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PEMBA (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>A supply train comes up from the lower depot in two days. Flour, kerosene, medical stores. I load it. I can hide one man among the crates, if he is small enough to fold and quiet enough to stay folded.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He measures Indy.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PEMBA (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You are not small. But you are the one who wants to read Kessler&#x27;s papers, I think. Not just watch his fence from a rock.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>How do you know what I want?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PEMBA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Because I have carried his papers for four years and never been permitted to read them. I am trusted to carry everything. I am trusted to see nothing.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>What do you want for this?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Pemba looks at his hands.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PEMBA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>My brother worked the depot road. Last September a German truck went off the switchback with crates that were not secured. My brother was walking beside it, because that was safer than riding on that grade. Except that day it was not. The camp paid his widow nothing. They said Tibetan roads were not their responsibility.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>His jaw works.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PEMBA (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I want it written down, somewhere your government will read it, with his name spelled correctly. I have carried his name wrong on my own tongue for nine months, because the Germans never asked how to say it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I can do that. More, if we get out with what I need.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PEMBA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Do not promise more than the first thing. I have had enough of men promising more than the first thing.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He stands.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PEMBA (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Come down after dark on the second night, where the trail forks below the big cairn. I will have a crate ready. It will not be comfortable.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I&#x27;ve ridden in worse.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PEMBA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Everyone says that. Until they try it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. RIDGE – NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A fire of yak dung that burns hot and smells like a barn. Tsering sharpens a knife that is already sharp.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He could be Kessler&#x27;s.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He could.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>A man who wants you to trust him will say he knew Norbu.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Yes.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>And you will go anyway.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>If he&#x27;s lying, I find out in a crate. If he&#x27;s not, I find out in a tent. Either way I find out.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She doesn&#x27;t argue. She measures out a box in stones on the ground, about the size of a coffin for a short man, and makes him practice.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INSERT – INDY FOLDING INTO THE STONE OUTLINE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He folds in. Folds out. She times him on his own watch.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (O.S.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Again. You make the noise of a cow lying down.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>By the eleventh try he can do it silently. On the twelfth he gets stuck, one knee behind his ear. She stands over him for a full minute considering the situation before she helps.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Do not do that on the day.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She sheathes the knife, takes a length of red wool cord from her pack, knotted in two places, and ties it round his left wrist without asking. The knots are small and hard. She pulls each one until it bites.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>What&#x27;s it for?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It is wool. It is warm.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She lets go.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Do not take it off in front of the Germans. They will want to look at it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. SUPPLY CRATE – MOVING – DAY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Black. Cramped. Smells of kerosene and flour. Indy, folded into a space built for neither, listens to a mule&#x27;s gait through inch-thick boards.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Two TAPS on the lid. Be still. Pemba, walking alongside, taps twice for most of the first hour, humming to the animals in a low flat tune, like a man talking to himself so he&#x27;ll have something to say if anyone asks.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. GERMAN CHECKPOINT – CONTINUOUS</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A mule train halts. A GERMAN SERGEANT walks down the line of crates counting aloud.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SERGEANT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Fünfundvierzig. Sechsundvierzig.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He stops at a crate chalked 47. Loses his place. Starts again from the beginning, annoyed.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. SUPPLY CRATE – CONTINUOUS</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Through a knothole Indy sees the next crate, chalked 47. He hears the count begin again, and hopes the man has a pencil.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. STORAGE TENT – SHOMO CAMP – DUSK</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The crate is set down hard enough to jar his teeth. He lies still a long while. Then he works the lid loose with a knife blade and climbs out into the smell of every place he has ever hidden.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Pemba returns with a German greatcoat, too tight in the shoulders, the insignia picked off, the threads still showing.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PEMBA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>From the laundry. A man who dies in the hospital tent does not need two.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The sleeves end an inch above Indy&#x27;s wrist. The red cord shows. He turns his cuff down over it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Through a gap in the tent flap, a slice of lit ground and the corner of the pen. A MAN IN A WHITE COAT crosses the gravel with a tin plate and a cup, a guard three paces behind. He sets the plate on a post by the wire, checks his watch, writes on a clipboard, and leaves.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>For a minute nothing moves.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Then the shape rises from the shadows, comes to the wire, and takes the plate in both hands.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>It eats carefully, with its fingers. When the plate is half empty, it stops. It pours the cup out onto the dirt, spoons the remaining rice into the empty cup with the edge of the plate, presses it down with a thumb, and carries it back into the dark.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy watches the place where it went. He couldn&#x27;t say why that is the thing that stops his breath. It put something by. Whatever is in that pen is thinking about tomorrow.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PEMBA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Kessler&#x27;s tent is third from the generator shed. He eats with the others at this hour every night, because he believes discipline is owed even to dinner. You have perhaps forty minutes.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>And the pen?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PEMBA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Guarded always. I would not go near it tonight, American. Whatever Kessler writes in his tent, he has written already. Whatever is in that pen has not finished happening yet.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Forty minutes.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. SHOMO CAMP – NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy crosses the camp in the dead man&#x27;s coat with a crate of tin cans on his shoulder, which is the only disguise anyone has ever needed in any army. Nobody stops him.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The mess tent is lit and loud. Cards snap on a table. A BAVARIAN complains about lice in detail. Another man answers with a slow impression of an officer: &quot;Kameraden. We are here to serve science.&quot; The tent roars. It takes Indy a moment to realize who is being mimicked. Not one of the generals.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Behind the mess tent, a clothesline holds three SS tunics, frozen stiff, arms out in the wind like men shot where they stood.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. KESSLER&#x27;S TENT – NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Bigger than the others. A surveyor&#x27;s table taking up half of it, maps pinned under brass weights. A cot with hospital corners. A toothbrush standing in a tin cup. A jar of boiled sweets with the lid screwed down tight. A tray with one clean cup and one turned upside down, as if Kessler expects company and has decided the guest will be a neat one.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Everything is square to everything else. Indy knew men like this in graduate school. They were usually the ones who cried at the oral exams.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He goes straight to the trunk at the foot of the cot, because spies learn early that men hide their worst secrets at ankle height. Locked. A pick he&#x27;s carried since Mexico opens it in thirty seconds.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Under a folded jacket with silver runes at the collar lies a leather folder stamped with the eagle and oak leaves.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He flips the pages. Skull indices, cheekbone breadth, the vocabulary of men who believed a brass caliper could sort humanity into what was worth keeping and what wasn&#x27;t. Under the tables, photographs pasted to grid sheets: Tibetan men on the same stool against the same canvas wall, calipers at their temples, placards on their chests.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>One stops him.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The man on the stool is about thirty. Broad-faced. A chipped front tooth. Under the placard, in pencil: N. Wangmo, guide. Spricht Englisch.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy doesn&#x27;t take it. A missing photograph is a thing a man notices. He puts it back exactly as it was and tells himself to remember the number.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Further in, Kessler&#x27;s handwriting loosens: a scientist writing for himself, not for Berlin. Indy reads, lips moving.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KESSLER (V.O.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(precise, dry)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The 1939 expedition located skeletal fragments in the ice field above the camp, suggestive beyond any specimen catalogued by Western science. Local informants confirmed an oral tradition of a living population, consistent across three valleys with no contact between them. One living animal was taken in a snare on the night before the survey ended, and did not survive the night. I was prevented from completing the survey in 1939 by circumstances outside the expedition&#x27;s control.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy reads the last phrase twice, the way he reads anything built to mean one thing on the surface and another underneath.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KESSLER (V.O.) (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Entry, 2 May. The specimen did not resist the net. It sat down in it. The men were disconcerted. I recorded the observation and instructed them not to repeat it in their reports.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He copies the two dated lines into his notebook, small and fast. The pencil snaps. He looks at the stub for half a second, then keeps the rest in his head, which is how he learned most of what he knows about the Sumerians.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>BOOTS on gravel.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He drops the folder in the trunk, shuts the lid, pinches out the lamp, and slides under the cot, cheek on the boards, as the flap lifts. A flashlight beam crosses the tent at ankle height. A SOLDIER. His boots are polished, which means he was told to.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The beam sweeps the table. Moves across the floor to the dropped padlock. Stops.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy counts. One. Two. The beam wavers. A spider runs across the back of his hand. He allows it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The soldier crosses to the table, unscrews the jar, takes out a sweet, puts it in his cheek. Whistles four bars through it. Leaves.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy lies there with his heart going. He snaps the padlock back on with fingers that won&#x27;t stop shaking. Reopens the trunk. Gives himself two more pages.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KESSLER (V.O.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The capture of a living specimen in April confirms what I observed in 1939. Preliminary examination suggests a surviving remnant population, exhibiting morphological characteristics that Reichsführer-SS Himmler&#x27;s office will find of the highest interest. I have withheld full reporting to Berlin pending—</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The flap moves.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy has time to drop the folder and get a hand on the lid before ERICH KESSLER steps inside: tall, younger than Indy pictured, pale eyes that take in the open trunk, the American beside it, and show none of the alarm the scene calls for.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KESSLER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You will be Jones. Please leave your hand where it is.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy&#x27;s hand has been drifting toward his revolver. It stops. Two armed men now fill the flap behind Kessler, rifles up. The arithmetic does not favor drawing.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I was looking for the latrine.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KESSLER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>In a locked trunk.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It&#x27;s a big camp.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KESSLER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It is a very small camp, Dr. Jones. I had it surveyed.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He closes the trunk himself, gently.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KESSLER (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Stand, please. My men are young and nervous, and I would rather not explain a dead American professor to Berlin before I have finished explaining the living specimen.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy&#x27;s revolver leaves his hip under a rifle muzzle and lands on the table with a sound too small for what it means to lose it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The bullwhip follows. Kessler lifts it between two fingers like a specimen, lays it beside the revolver, and squares the pair to the table&#x27;s edge.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Kessler doesn&#x27;t sit. He goes round the tent putting right everything Indy touched, the trunk lid, the brass weights, the jar, like a man tidying after a burglary while delivering a speech he has rehearsed for months to an empty tent.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KESSLER (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I expected a thief. I did not expect to be glad of one. Berlin wants a root-race discovery, something Himmler can show the Führer. In Berlin&#x27;s hands my specimen becomes a photograph, and then a skull in a case. What I lack is a credentialed scholar from outside the Reich who will examine him and put his name to what he is, somewhere Berlin cannot correct it. Someone is coming from Berlin to supervise me. I would like the question settled before he arrives.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy nudges a brass weight a quarter inch out of line. Kessler, mid-sentence, without looking, nudges it back.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You could have written to the Royal Asiatic Society.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KESSLER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I did. They replied they had no one qualified and did not care to be associated with the Institute.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He lifts his chin.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KESSLER (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I have also read your article on the Ur-Nammu tablets, Dr. Jones. Your second footnote is wrong. The sign is Old Babylonian, not Sargonic. It affects your whole argument.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You came three thousand miles to tell me my footnote&#x27;s wrong?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KESSLER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I came for other reasons. The footnote I noticed on the voyage. I have had four years of men who nod when I speak.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>There is no humor in it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KESSLER (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I find it tiresome.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It&#x27;s Sargonic.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KESSLER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It is not.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I&#x27;ll send you a corrected copy, if we both live.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Kessler very nearly smiles. Then he sees that Indy has turned the jar a quarter turn, and the smile goes away.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You want me to authenticate your Nazi science.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KESSLER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I want you to look at what is in that pen and tell me honestly, as the scholar you are rather than the soldier your government made of you this week, what you believe it to be. I think you will find the honest answer considerably more complicated than Berlin wants to hear.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He takes a boiled sweet from the jar and holds it out. Indy shakes his head.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KESSLER (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>My sister sends them from Zurich once a month. She believes I am in the Black Forest studying moss. You may think what you like of me. I did not choose to be good at this.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You haven&#x27;t tried. That&#x27;s different.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Kessler sucks the sweet and says nothing. For a moment he looks like what he must have been at twelve: a boy who took a wrong turn into a book of birds.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Does he have a name?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KESSLER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Specimens are catalogued by number.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Then you haven&#x27;t asked.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KESSLER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I do not speak the language.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He squares the jar one last time and nods at the flap.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KESSLER (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Come. It is a short walk. The night air up here is quite bracing, once you have stopped minding the cold.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. THE PEN – NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Kessler doesn&#x27;t hurry. He takes a lantern from the guard at the tent door and carries it himself. The camp is nearly empty. Everyone who can be at dinner is at dinner.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The pen is bigger up close. Shoring as thick as a man&#x27;s thigh, bolted at the corners with iron straps, a second line of wire along the top. Kessler raises the lantern and stands aside.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KESSLER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Do not go closer than the wire.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I wasn&#x27;t planning to.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KESSLER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Of course. Look, please.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>It sits against the far timber with its knees drawn up. Taller than a man, wide through the shoulder, covered everywhere but the face and palms in reddish-brown hair.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The face is what Indy braced for, and it still gets past him. It isn&#x27;t a man&#x27;s face and it isn&#x27;t an animal&#x27;s. Heavy brow. Deep-set eyes. Mouth shut tight. It looks at the lantern, then at Indy, with an attention he has seen on exactly one other kind of face: a patient in a long ward who has stopped expecting anyone to explain.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KESSLER (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Tell me what you see.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>A prisoner.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KESSLER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That is not an anatomical description.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It&#x27;s the only one I&#x27;m sure of. How long have you had it in that hole?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KESSLER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Seven weeks. He eats well. We have given him a roof for the rain.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He. Kessler said it the way you say it about a dog you&#x27;ve decided to like. Indy lets it go by, because his eyes have found something on the nearest post, at the height of a seated man&#x27;s reach.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Scratches. Deep, made with something hard. They run in groups of five: four short strokes and a long one across. Row under row.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He counts. Forty-nine.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Groups of five. Four short strokes and one long across. He has seen that tally before, cut into a stone two days below the pass, under a thousand years of lichen. Why count a border? To know when someone crosses. He had thought she meant people.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Kessler follows his look. When he speaks it isn&#x27;t in the voice he used in the tent.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KESSLER (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I told the guards it was random. A habit. Sharpening claws. I have not been able to make myself believe that for some weeks.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Seven weeks is forty-nine days. He&#x27;s counting.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KESSLER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Or marking something. I would much rather have a man who is not on my payroll tell me it means nothing.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy looks at the tally for a long time. His eye slides down the post to the pen&#x27;s lower corner, where an iron strap runs across the shoring, held by four bolts. Two of the bolt heads are bright where the paint has worn, as though someone has been handling them.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He notes it and moves on, because he is looking at a man&#x27;s count of his own captivity and couldn&#x27;t look at anything else.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Behind the wire the creature has put its chin on its knees and is no longer watching either of them.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I can&#x27;t tell you that.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KESSLER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>No. I was afraid not.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. SUPPLY TENT – NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Guarded, but not mistreated. Kessler still has a use for him. Around midnight the guard at the flap, a kid of eighteen with a scab on his chin, brings in a tin of cold stew, sets it on a crate, and doesn&#x27;t leave.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>YOUNG GUARD</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Herr Professor. You have seen the snow-man?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I&#x27;ve seen a lot of snow.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>YOUNG GUARD</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>No. The man. Hauptmann says it is a bear. Corporal Meyer says it is a man in a skin. A prisoner. He says the Hauptmann is lying.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>What do you say?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The boy looks at the stew, the flap, Indy.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>YOUNG GUARD</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I say I would like to go home. I say it is very big.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Eat your stew.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>It was Indy&#x27;s own stew. The boy, to his own visible surprise, does.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. SHOMO CAMP – DAWN</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>They let him go at first light on Kessler&#x27;s instruction: the Herr Professor is to go away and think, and come back when he has.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>On his way out, the MESS COOK, a stout man with a pipe and no apparent interest in the war, steps from his tent and puts a heel of black bread in Indy&#x27;s hand. He says something in German. Indy takes it for a blessing, or a complaint about the bread.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. SLOPE ABOVE THE CAMP – CONTINUOUS</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Halfway up, Indy hears the DOG. A big yellow mongrel on a lead, coming down the track with a SERGEANT and a PRIVATE behind it. There is no cover but one shoulder-high rock. He gets behind it and lies still.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The dog stops. Lifts its head. Turns, with the calm of an animal that has found the interesting thing, toward the rock.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SERGEANT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Was ist?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy tears the bread in half and tosses it six feet to the side. The dog goes after it. The private laughs, tired, and says the cook&#x27;s bread is good enough for a dog and not for a soldier.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The dog looks back at the rock, plainly unfinished. Indy throws the second half.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The sergeant looks at the dog. At the bread. For one second, directly at the rock. Indy sees the gray in his stubble.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Then he looks away.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>SERGEANT (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Komm.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Quite gently. The patrol goes on down toward the camp.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>It&#x27;s the second time in a day that someone in that camp has decided not to see him. He doesn&#x27;t know what to do with the fact.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. RIDGE ABOVE SHOMO – DAY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Tsering is waiting, three days of watching having worn a groove of worry into a face that doesn&#x27;t usually show much.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You&#x27;re alive.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>From her, it is close to an embrace.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He tells her everything on the climb back. The trunk. The report. Kessler&#x27;s offer. The unfinished sentence. The tallies. She mends his sheepskin coat on her knee while he talks, with a bone needle, and the needle goes in a little harder with each piece of news. At Kessler&#x27;s offer it goes through the cloth and into her thumb. She sucks it, unbothered, and keeps sewing.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The photograph he tells last, because it is the only thing he isn&#x27;t sure she&#x27;ll want.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Say the name under it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>N. Wangmo. Guide. Speaks English.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Did he have a tooth gone? On the left, in front.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Yes.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She nods slowly and says nothing for a long time. A RAVEN lands on the rock above them, looks, and leaves.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He learned English from a missionary who was kind to him. He was proud of it. I used to tell him his accent was a joke.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She picks a thread from her sleeve.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I would like to see the picture. When this is finished.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>If I can get it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You will not. Men like Kessler keep what they take. But I would like to see it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>&quot;Circumstances outside the expedition&#x27;s control.&quot; He didn&#x27;t finish the sentence before he caught me. I think it&#x27;s Norbu.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Don&#x27;t.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>It comes out sharp, a blade drawn fast.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Do not tell me what I know, American. You have been in this valley six days. I have carried this four years.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Then help me finish it. Norbu&#x27;s last letter. The one before the news.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>There was not a last letter.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Too fast.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Tsering.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The wind rattles the prayer flags behind them. She fights something out on her own face she would plainly have preferred to fight in private. Indy says, before she can speak:</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I pushed too hard. You don&#x27;t owe me Norbu.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>No. I don&#x27;t.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She pulls her coat tighter, though the cold isn&#x27;t why.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>There was a letter. Not through the trading company&#x27;s post. A porter leaving the expedition two weeks early brought it to me. I have had it four years. I have read it exactly once.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Why once?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Because once was enough.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>From an inner pocket sewn with a lifetime of careful mending she draws a folded square of paper gone soft and gray at the creases.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I was going to burn it. A dozen times. I never did. I don&#x27;t know why.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You know why.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Yes.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She holds it out. Her hand doesn&#x27;t shake, which costs her more than if it had.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Read it to me. Every time I try, the Tibetan runs together and I can&#x27;t make the letters stay in order. He wrote half of it in English, the half he wanted the Germans not to understand if it was searched.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy unfolds it with the care he gives anything old.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>NORBU (V.O.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(warm, quick, a man writing against the clock)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Sister, I am writing this quickly and giving it to a man leaving tonight whom I trust more than the post. They found bones in the ice above the camp three weeks ago. Kessler has not told the others everything he believes about them. I helped him dig.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>FLASHBACK – EXT. ICE FIELD ABOVE SHOMO – NIGHT (1939)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Blue-black moonlight on ice. NORBU WANGMO, thirty-one, broad-faced, a chipped front tooth, crouches behind a ridge. Below him, a YOUNGER KESSLER and two men, LANG and BRENNER, set snares meant for something larger than any animal in these mountains.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>NORBU (V.O.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Two nights ago I followed him and two of his men past the ice field after dark. I watched them set snares meant for something larger than any animal I know in these mountains. I did not understand until I saw what came into one before dawn, crying out in a voice with words in it, or close enough to words that no man who heard it could call it only an animal.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Norbu&#x27;s face, white in the dark. He does not look afraid. He looks like a man about to laugh at something terrible.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Below him, Kessler turns. Looks straight up the slope. Straight at him.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>NORBU (V.O.) (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Kessler saw me watching. He has changed toward me, as you do toward someone you cannot afford to trust. I will try to leave early, on the excuse of a family matter. If something happens to me before I can, know that I did not fall on any slope I did not know by heart. Know that whatever they tell you, it was not the mountain that—</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Transition"><Text>CUT BACK TO:</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. RIDGE ABOVE SHOMO – DAY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The letter stops there.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Not a trailing thought. The pen simply came off the page, the last word cut at its first stroke. Beneath it, a long tear runs up from the bottom edge, old and soft and brown, where the page has been folded and unfolded so many times along exactly that line that it has finally given way.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(quietly)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It was the mountain that. He never finished it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I have known what the next word was for four years.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>When her voice breaks, it breaks the way ice breaks on a lake in spring, all at once, with a sound that carries farther than either of them expects.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Killed. It was the mountain that killed him. And it was Kessler who decided the mountain would.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. ROCK OVERHANG – NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A small fire, risked in the lee of the boulders. Tsering sits with Norbu&#x27;s letter in her lap, not looking at it. When she speaks, her voice has gone back to the flat control Indy first heard in Rateng, as though what broke earlier was a door that opened once and shut itself hard behind her.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Kessler is down there sleeping in that tent with his unfinished sentences. I want to go down tonight and put a knife in him and leave before anyone finds him.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>If you do, the specimen stays in that pen. Kessler&#x27;s report gets finished by someone just as willing to write it, with less conscience about it. I promise you that&#x27;s possible. I&#x27;ve met the type.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He keeps his voice level.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Your brother&#x27;s letter doesn&#x27;t end with Kessler dead in his tent. It ends with the truth getting out. And if you go down that slope tonight, the only one who dies for certain is you.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She doesn&#x27;t answer right away. Far down the valley a dog or a wolf begins a long rising call that nobody answers.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I am not leaving. Whatever you think I decided, deciding not to put a knife in a sleeping man is not the same as being finished. I am not leaving this valley until that man answers for what he did, in words, in front of someone who can make it matter.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I know.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Good.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She lies down at last, not next to him but near enough that her back is to his, the single blanket stretched across them both like a bad treaty.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(into the dark)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The woman on the dock. In your other country. Did you keep your promise?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Part of it. I told her the truth. I didn&#x27;t do it fast enough.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That is the part you break most often. Men tell the truth when it is too late to be used.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I&#x27;ll try to do better.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You will not. But you will notice. That is something.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The blanket slips. She pulls it back over his shoulder without turning around. Neither of them mentions it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. RIDGE – DAWN</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Gray light, no warmth. Tsering has Indy cycling the bolt of her father&#x27;s LEE-ENFIELD, her hand over his. Hurried footsteps on scree: PEMBA, out of breath, moving faster than Indy has ever seen him move.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Keep going. He can talk to your back.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PEMBA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Berlin. A signal came in last night. Someone is coming to take charge, and the extraction is moved forward. There is a landing strip two valleys east, where the Luftwaffe has been flying in supplies. They will fly the specimen and the reports out in two days, not two weeks.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>His hands won&#x27;t stay still.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PEMBA (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>And Kessler has ordered the rocket troop reactivated. The ones with the harness packs. I did not know we had such men until this morning. They were kept in a separate tent near the shed, and I was told never to go near it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The bolt slams home with a CLACK like a dropped plate. Tsering raps Indy&#x27;s knuckles.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Slowly.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(working the bolt, gently now)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The ones from the plane. Or more of them.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PEMBA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Eight men came out of that tent carrying equipment I had never seen. Kessler watched them looking pleased for the first time. The young one in charge was whistling. He asked whether the mountain behind the valley had a name in my language. I told him. Lhari. He said it back correctly. He was polite.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He looks at Indy as though that were the worst part.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PEMBA (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>They are all so polite.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Tsering takes the rifle back.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>If you do it fast, it speaks. If you do it slowly, it only breathes.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Why not take your own rifle?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It is the only thing of my father&#x27;s that Norbu did not want. He said it kicked too hard. He said it was not a gun for a man who talks to mules. You will never use it. But you will know how it sounds when someone else does.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. DRY LAKE BED – THE AIRSTRIP – DAY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A long scrape of gravel on a dry lake bed. A flour sack on a pole for a windsock. At the near end, nose to the wind, a JUNKERS 52 with the swastika on its tail painted over in a gray that doesn&#x27;t quite take. Petrol drums stacked in rows beside it. Six men in sheepskins smoke on top of them.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy and Tsering watch from a gully above.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Burn it?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Then Kessler sends for another and moves the clock up even more.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He counts the men. Six. The drums. Forty-one.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>We don&#x27;t touch it. We just need to know it&#x27;s there.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>They&#x27;ve been watching an hour when the sound begins: a high thin SCREAMING, far off. He knows it before he knows he knows it. He rolls onto his back.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Two shapes are coming over the eastern ridge, low, trailing flame, following their own tracks in the shale.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Run.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>They can fly. We can&#x27;t run. But we can pick where.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A cut in the shale wall forty yards south, six feet wide, a ledge leaning over it like a shelf.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He takes her arm. They go down in a rattle of loose stone and slide under the overhang with the rifle between them as the flames come on.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. THE GULLY – CONTINUOUS</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The rocket troopers HOVER above the gully, their harnesses shrieking. Heat drips meltwater off the rock onto Indy&#x27;s neck.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He puts his watch face up on his knee and begins to count.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(whispering)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I read the bulletin in London. Ninety seconds at full climb. A hover burns faster.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>What?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I&#x27;m counting.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A boot comes down past the lip. Then a leg. Then gloved hands working a valve. Tsering&#x27;s rifle comes up. Indy puts his palm on the barrel and pushes it down. She gives him a look of pure fury. He shakes his head once.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Twenty seconds. Forty. A gout of fire comes down the gully and the shale cracks. Sixty. A tongue of flame licks along the overhang an arm&#x27;s length from his head. Seventy. Eighty. He can smell burning leather.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He finds, to his surprise, that he is praying.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Ninety-three seconds.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The second pack COUGHS: an ugly stutter in the fire. The trooper drops six feet, catches himself, drops again. The first swings wildly to help. Both go away over the ridge in a spiral of black smoke, shouting. A moment later comes a distant crash, and then nothing.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Tsering lowers the rifle. She is breathing through her teeth.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>How did you know?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I read a bulletin.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>A paper.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She says it as though he&#x27;d pulled a coin from behind her ear.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You counted. You waited. With a rifle on my knee.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That&#x27;s what I do. I read things and then I get lucky.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It is not luck to count.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She slaps shale from her coat.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Eight who fly. Six, now. They will be back with more.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>We can&#x27;t take on the camp.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>We do not need to take it. We need to be inside for as long as it takes to do one thing. Two days. Then they fly out, and my brother&#x27;s name is a hole in the ground.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>From the valley, faint and regular, the sound of mule bells. Indy realizes he&#x27;s counting them without meaning to, the way a man counts the ticks of a clock in a sickroom.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Two days.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>And until then?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Kessler&#x27;s report says &quot;skeletal fragments in the ice field above the camp.&quot; Norbu&#x27;s letter says bones in the ice. A glacier doesn&#x27;t keep bones. Somebody puts them there.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(after a long moment)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The place the herders will not graze.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Where do we start?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>At dawn. Not before. That place does not like to be found in the dark.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. THE ICE FIELD ABOVE SHOMO – DAWN</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The glacier is a river that stopped in mid-sentence. Gray, ridged, scoured by a wind with nothing else to do. Above it the cliffs of Lhari go straight up into cloud. Far below, the German camp is a handful of crumbs.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy and Tsering climb roped together, picks in their fists. The mules have been left in a hollow at the glacier&#x27;s foot with the honest gray in charge. Tsering walks the way a woman walks into a house she has refused to enter for four years.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Halfway up they pass a ruin that isn&#x27;t old enough to be one. A German dig, 1939: three pits blasted into the ice, ringed with rusted pickets. A surveyor&#x27;s tripod lies on its side like a dead heron. A shred of tent flap snaps in the wind, an eagle on it so faded it seems to be saying one small thing over and over. Go away. Go away.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Tsering stops at the largest pit. A tent pole lies across it. Cut into the wood, small and deep, by someone who couldn&#x27;t leave a surface alone: N.W.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Is that—</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He carved his name in everything. Our father&#x27;s plough. The monastery door. He was beaten for the door.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She steps over the pole without touching it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He was proud of the door.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>They slide down into the pit. In the blue wall at the bottom: bones. Long ones, ribs, a jaw as heavy as a boot. Indy goes to his knees and brushes the frost away with his glove.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>They aren&#x27;t scattered. They&#x27;re laid. Side by side, small to large, in the ice.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Kessler&#x27;s &quot;skeletal fragments.&quot; Nobody dropped these. A glacier doesn&#x27;t sort.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Then someone mourned them.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He looks up out of the pit. Climbing the ice toward the cliff, half-buried to the knee, a double line of standing stones runs up the glacier like the teeth of a comb. The same long-armed figures. Hands raised, palms out. Eleven pairs, and under each pair, a tally of strokes.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It&#x27;s the same road.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It is a road.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It&#x27;s a road that goes in.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>They follow it up, counting the stones under their breath, and stop where the double row ends against a wall of ice a good deal flatter than ice has any reason to be. Gray. Blank. Nothing.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>There is nothing here.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy checks his watch. Then the eastern ridge.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Wait for the sun.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>They wait. Indy bounces on his toes. Tsering stands like one of the stones.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The sun clears the ridge. A long gold blade sweeps across the glacier and strikes the wall at a low angle, and the wall lights from inside, like a candle behind a curtain. Deep in the ice, sharp-edged and perfect: a doorway. Two upright slabs and a lintel. Across the lintel, enormous, two carved hands, palms out, glowing like fossils in amber.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(barely)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Kessler dug within a hundred yards of this for three weeks.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He dug all day. Nobody ever waits for morning.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>It takes six minutes and Tsering&#x27;s adze to open a window in the ice. Behind it: a single slab of black stone, taller than a man, set in a frame of the same stone. At shoulder height, two carved palms, hollowed pale by something that isn&#x27;t weather. No handle. No hinge. No keyhole.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy lays his hands on the carved palms. Nothing. He pushes. Nothing. Tsering puts her shoulder to the stone beside him. Nothing. Two people at seventeen thousand feet, pushing on a door.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Everyone pushes.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That&#x27;s what it&#x27;s for. It&#x27;s built to be pushed by people in a hurry.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He steps back. Crouches. At the foot of the slab, at knuckle height, a vertical line of strokes. Four short. One long across.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>&quot;We&#x27;d like you to knock.&quot;</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You said that at the stones.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I was being clever.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>And now?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Now I&#x27;m being polite.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He knocks. Four short raps with his knuckles. For the fifth he lays the heel of his hand against the stone and lets it fall: one long, flat, heavy blow.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The sound goes into the mountain and does not come back.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Silence. Then a tick, high in the slab. A hairline of frost races down the seam beside the frame, ping, ping, ping, ping, like a zipper made of glass. Something enormous lets go inside the wall. The slab sinks into the floor with a groan older than any religion, and a breath of air flows out over their faces: warm, smelling of barley smoke and old stone.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>And then the note. Deep, bronze, rolling out of the doorway and across the ice field, down the glacier, over the camp, filling the valley like a bell the size of a mountain. It goes on for ten seconds. When it dies, the silence after it feels like a held breath.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Tsering looks down the glacier.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Everyone heard that.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It&#x27;s a doorbell.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It is a very large doorbell.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It&#x27;s polite to ring it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Far below, thin and rising, a sound Indy knows too well: the scream of rocket fuel, climbing.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The Germans are also polite.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Ten minutes.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Then we do not stay.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>No.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He is already through the door.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. THE HALL OF COUNTING – CONTINUOUS</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A gallery cut into the flank of the mountain, tall as a church and no wider than a street. The roof is the glacier itself: ten feet of blue ice, through which the morning comes down like light under water. Everything is blue and gold.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>And on every wall, floor to ceiling, as far as the light will go: the strokes. Four short, one long across. Four short, one long across. Row under row under row. Not hundreds. Not thousands. Hundreds of thousands, cut by a thousand different hands, the oldest by the door worn to ghosts, the newest, far down the gallery, sharp as the day. Between the rows, pressed into the stone in red ochre and charcoal: palm prints. A child&#x27;s. A giant&#x27;s. A child&#x27;s.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Along the foot of the walls runs a line of niches, each holding one small thing: a comb, a bead, a bronze bell, a pair of felt slippers. Beside each, a stone bowl.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Low strings. A choir that isn&#x27;t sure it&#x27;s allowed.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy has stopped breathing. It is the sound of a man who has crawled through a great many holes in the ground and has just found the one he&#x27;ll be lying about at dinner parties for the rest of his life.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(a whisper)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Oh.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Do not touch anything.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I&#x27;m not touching anything.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He is touching the wall, lightly, under the strokes, the way a man reads Braille.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It&#x27;s a ledger. Every group is a visit. Four short strokes, four days. And the long one across the end—</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Is the fifth.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(laughing, helplessly)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The fifth day, you go. Four days&#x27; welcome and then, with great affection, out. Hospitality with a deadline. It&#x27;s the most civilized thing I&#x27;ve ever seen.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It is a good rule. My mother had it for relatives.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>They walk the wall. Each row begins with the same small carving: a hand.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Everyone who came in was counted in. They knocked, and they were counted.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>And the Germans?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>There&#x27;s no row for the Germans.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>They were here in thirty-nine.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>They never knocked. They&#x27;re not guests. They&#x27;re not even enemies. They&#x27;re not counted at all. They&#x27;re weather.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That is worse.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Much worse.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>At the far end of the gallery, where the walls are freshest, he finds a column cut so recently that stone dust still lies in the grooves. Groups of five, one under another. He counts under his breath. Ten groups. And two.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Fifty-two.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He turns, slowly.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Fifty-two nights. It&#x27;s the pen. He&#x27;s been marking the post, and somebody here has been keeping the same count.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He touches the last stroke. It is white as fresh bone.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>This one&#x27;s from this morning.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(very quietly)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Then someone was here before us.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>They both look into the dark behind them. Nothing moves. Somewhere, water drips.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Kessler wrote that it didn&#x27;t resist the net. It sat down in it. I took that for surrender.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He swallows.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He didn&#x27;t get caught, Tsering. He arrived. He went to a stranger&#x27;s door and sat down on the step. And they put him in a hole.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>And his people are counting.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Every night. Waiting for the fifth day. Waiting to see if their guest comes home.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He stops, because Tsering has stopped.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She has walked to the last niche, at the end of the wall, and stands in front of it with her hands at her sides. In it: a battered black felt hat, its brim bent by someone else&#x27;s years. Beside it, a stone bowl of barley, pressed down smooth with a thumb. The barley is fresh. It still gleams.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Tucked under the hat&#x27;s band, a loop of red wool cord. Knotted twice. Small and hard.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy&#x27;s eyes go, without his permission, to his own left wrist.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Tsering doesn&#x27;t touch the hat. She puts her hand flat against the stone beside the niche, the way you steady yourself on a doorframe.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(barely)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He lost it in every valley in Tibet. It was the only hat that fit him. He had a very large head.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(gently)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It&#x27;s a guest&#x27;s token. You leave something at the door. You take it back when you go.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He did not go.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>No.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She looks at the bowl.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>They have been feeding him.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Her voice does something she has not permitted.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Four years. Every day. They have been keeping his place.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy has nothing to say, and for once knows it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I told the whole valley he was dead. I built a cairn.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A breath.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Nobody counted him out.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A PHOTOGRAPHIC FLASH.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>White, enormous. The Hall leaps out of the dark: every stroke, every handprint, a million shadows flung up the walls, and in the middle two figures thrown into silhouette. Tsering&#x27;s knife is in her hand before the light dies. Indy&#x27;s whip is in his.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JO (O.S.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Don&#x27;t shoot. Photographer.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A woman in the doorway, black against the blue: a SPEED GRAPHIC slung across her chest like a bazooka, a Colt .45 in her free hand that she has the manners not to point at anyone.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>JOSEPHINE &quot;JO&quot; CALDER is around thirty, in a flight jacket two sizes too big and a fur-lined cap with copper hair escaping from under it, freckles across a nose that was broken once and improved by it, and a mouth that looks as if it has just thought of something it shouldn&#x27;t say.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy knows the mouth. He last saw it in a Cairo bar on a Tuesday neither of them discusses.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Jo.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JO</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Indiana. You look terrible. Where&#x27;s the hat?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>A yak ate it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JO</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Of course it did.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>What are you doing here?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JO</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Following you since Rateng. The trail was a joke. You left a sock.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(not looking at him)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I told you.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JO</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Donovan wanted pictures. Shaw wanted a witness and didn&#x27;t tell you, because Shaw never tells anybody anything. I wanted to watch you make an ass of yourself on a mountain.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She turns to Tsering and offers a hand, which Tsering looks at.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JO (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Josephine Calder, LIFE. I&#x27;d say it&#x27;s a pleasure, but your gray mule bit me.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He is a very honest mule. And I knew. Since the first pass. I wanted to see if you could follow my animals as far as the fence.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You knew?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You were busy being slow.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JO</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Is that a compliment?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It is arithmetic.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Jo takes in the Hall. The camera comes halfway up. Stops.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JO</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(very small)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Oh, my God.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>And then they all hear it. Not down the glacier now. Above. The scream of rocket packs, close, and riding on top of it, perfectly clear in the thin cold air, someone WHISTLING. A cheerful tune. Nearly a waltz.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JO (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Friends of yours?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>They were on my plane.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JO</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Of course they were.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy has a sheet of the rubbing paper out and is working his pencil over the newest column as fast as it will go.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Now.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>One more row—</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Now.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She lifts the hat from its niche the way you lift a sleeping child, and holds it against her chest.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(quietly)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That&#x27;s the fifth day, you know. Taking his token. That&#x27;s him leaving.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Then he leaves.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>High above them the blue roof turns orange.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The troopers are hovering directly over the Hall in the glare of their own flames, and the ice is answering. A crack runs the length of the ceiling like lightning drawn in slow motion. Meltwater begins to rain.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JO</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That&#x27;s not good.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It&#x27;s very not good.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>CRUMP. A satchel charge goes off on the glacier. A shaft of raw daylight punches through the roof and two black figures drop through it, harness flames roaring, and land on the gallery floor forty feet away, between them and the door. They don&#x27;t look at the walls. They look at targets.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A pistol shot takes a bite out of the carved wall, and a thousand years of strokes go with it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Don&#x27;t shoot the wall!</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You are worried about the wall?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JO</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Run!</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The troopers lift off the floor to close the distance. And the ice above them, cracked by their own heat, lets go. A blue wall falls across the gallery. The first trooper goes flat. The second turns to look back, mouth open, and a slab the size of a wagon comes down across him, and his flame goes out like a pinched candle.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>It isn&#x27;t done. The whole roof is coming down in sections, from the hole outward, each block hitting the floor like a hammer on an anvil, in order, coming. The Hall of Counting is being erased, a century a second, and the way out lies straight through it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>They run. Ice the size of pianos lands to left and right. Tsering, hat clutched to her chest. Jo, camera over her head. Indy last, the rubbing clenched in his teeth.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Jo goes down. The Speed Graphic strap has caught on a ledge. A block of ice the size of a wagon is falling toward her. CRACK. The whip wraps her waist. Indy hauls, and she skates out from under it on her stomach, and the block shatters where she was.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JO</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Thanks!</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(through the paper)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Don&#x27;t mention it!</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Ahead, the great slab is rising, grinding up out of its slot. A mountain that has kept its door open for four years is closing it. A gap of five feet. Four.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Tsering dives through, rolls, the hat still against her chest. Jo goes under on her back with the camera held over her like a baby. The gap is three feet. Two.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy is last. And Indy, God help him, is looking back. The wall. The last column, fifty-two strokes, vanishing under the blue.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (O.S.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Indiana!</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He drops and slides. The slab comes down. It takes the sole off his left boot and the rubbing from his teeth, and shuts with a sound like the end of an argument.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. THE ICE FIELD – CONTINUOUS</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>They tumble out onto the glacier as the cliff comes down behind them. Not a collapse so much as a long, soft exhale: the wall of ice folds over the doorway like a hand closing, and the glacier, in ten seconds, heals. Where the Hall of Counting was, there is a slope of blue-white rubble any geologist would swear had lain there ten thousand years.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Above them, flames circle the new crater once, twice, like gulls over a boat. The whistling has stopped. For a long moment there is only wind. Then it begins again. Slower. Lower. A man walking home from something. The flames drift away toward the camp.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The three of them lie in the snow, breathing. Indy is on his back. He is shaking, and not from the cold.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That was the best thing I&#x27;ll ever find.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JO</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It was.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>And I never wrote it down.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JO</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(checking the Speed Graphic)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I got one. One exposure. The rest of my film is in a bag under forty tons of ice.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She looks at the camera.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JO (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It&#x27;s the only picture I&#x27;ve ever taken that I&#x27;m afraid to look at.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Tsering gets to her feet with the hat in both hands. She turns it once. Straightens the brim. Takes the red cord out of the band, looks at it, and puts it back.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You&#x27;re carrying that.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It needs carrying.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She puts it in her pack. Then she walks to the nearest standing stone, where a tally runs down the weathered face, and with the point of her knife cuts a long stroke across the lowest four.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Four days. And the fifth.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She sheathes the knife.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He is counted out.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Then, because she is Tsering:</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>We are late. Get up.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. RIDGE ABOVE SHOMO – AFTERNOON</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Tsering shaves Indy with her knife, on the theory that a man who walks into a German camp looking like a yak will be shot like one. Jo sits on a rock cleaning her lens with the hem of her shirt, an attachment nobody authorized and nobody has yet found the nerve to terminate. Pemba watches the camp through field glasses.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PEMBA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The troop is four now, not six. Two did not come back from the glacier. Their leader walked the length of the camp whistling. Nobody dared ask him to stop.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PEMBA (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Albrecht Voss. Four soldiers, a wireless set, and a case marked FRAGILE that three men had to carry. A porter asked what was in it. A soldier said, very quietly, &quot;Records.&quot; The camp calls him the Doctor from Berlin, though nobody knows what he is a doctor of.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Administration. Every institute like Kessler&#x27;s has one. Somebody who signs the checks and reads the reports Kessler would rather not write.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PEMBA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Since he came, nothing is the way it was. He sent back the water because it was not boiled twice. He sent back the bed. He has a gramophone, and in the evening he plays a man singing, very slowly, very sad. The soldiers hate it. Kessler asked him to stop and he said Kessler&#x27;s authority did not extend to the acoustics of the officers&#x27; quarters.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He said that?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PEMBA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>In the passive voice.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He looks pleased, and a little ashamed to be pleased.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PEMBA (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I did not know there was a way to be angry at a man in the passive voice. It is very effective. Nobody could tell who it was meant for.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy laughs. Tsering&#x27;s knife nicks him.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Hold still.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>No sympathy at all.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I want to know what Berlin actually wants from this valley. I don&#x27;t think it&#x27;s what Kessler wants. A crack like that is usually the only door worth walking through.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You are going to walk in the front door.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The side door. North perimeter.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That is a door.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She turns to Pemba. They trade a few lines in Tibetan in which Indy catches the word for bell.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PEMBA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The cook rings the dinner bell at seven. She says if you are not out of the camp by the seventh bell, she will come in after you.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>She&#x27;ll be shot.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PEMBA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>She says yes. She says it is not a threat. It is a schedule.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Seventh bell. Don&#x27;t be early.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I will be exactly on time. You will be the one who is late.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. SHOMO CAMP – NORTH PERIMETER – DUSK</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy walks in alone, no crate this time, hands visible. A GUARD challenges him forty feet in and marches him at rifle point toward the largest tent, which tells him Voss&#x27;s arrival has changed the camp&#x27;s rules on what a wandering American is worth shooting over.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>They take his belt, his pocketknife, and the red cord from his wrist. A young CORPORAL turns the cord over twice, shrugs, and hands it back as if it were a child&#x27;s toy.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. VOSS&#x27;S TENT – DUSK</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Warm. A real iron stove, a kettle, and real coffee, not the roasted acorn the Reich has been drinking for two years. Somebody flew it in, deciding a man from Berlin was worth the weight. On the camp table, turned so the man in the chair can see it, stands a photograph of a plump woman in a hat with two small girls.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>VOSS (O.S.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Kuhn. The ribbon is in the second drawer, not the first. It was in the second drawer yesterday.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A CORPORAL named KUHN at the door flinches, closes his ledger, and shows Indy in with the face of a man who spends most of his day being corrected.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>ALBRECHT VOSS is already on his feet, in his coat, with a clipboard: fifties, thinning hair combed flat, the unhurried patience of someone who has spent a career outlasting more excitable men. He doesn&#x27;t offer his hand.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>VOSS</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Walk with me, Dr. Jones. I inspect at dusk, and I will not be late on account of a visitor.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He&#x27;s through the flap before Indy can answer, Kuhn trotting behind with the ledger.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. SHOMO CAMP – CONTINUOUS</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The strangest interrogation Indy has ever sat through, because nobody sits. Voss marches him across the camp at a clip and conducts his business and Indy&#x27;s at once.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He stops at a sentry with an open top button. &quot;Name.&quot; Writes it down. Says nothing else, which turns the sentry the color of a boiled ham. He kicks a tent peg. It holds. He looks faintly disappointed.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>VOSS</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Kessler speaks of you with professional respect, which from him is rare. He believes you are the only man within a thousand miles qualified to confirm his findings. I read his summary. It had a typographical error on page four, in the word &quot;skeletal.&quot; A man who cannot control his typists cannot control a specimen.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I&#x27;ve formed an opinion of what&#x27;s in that pen.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>VOSS</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You imagine Berlin wants proof. A living ancestor. That is Kessler&#x27;s dream.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He says it flatly, without the caution Indy would expect under a regime that punishes frankness.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>VOSS (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Berlin&#x27;s dream is winning a war it is no longer certain of winning. I require a specimen photogenic enough for a headline and a report clean enough that no journalist can prove it a fraud. What I will not permit to leave this valley is a complication.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Like a dead guide in nineteen thirty-nine.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Voss&#x27;s face doesn&#x27;t move, which is its own kind of answer. He stops at a sagging guy rope, thrusts the clipboard at Indy, &quot;Hold this,&quot; and kneels to retie the knot, muttering.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>VOSS</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Kessler&#x27;s methods in thirty-nine were his own affair. If a guide died inconveniently four years ago, that is a matter for Kessler&#x27;s conscience, assuming he retains one, and not for any report bearing my signature. I have no interest in unburying it, which amounts to the same practical result.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy, who was raised never to pass an unguarded pencil, slides the one from under the clip up his sleeve.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>VOSS (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I would also prefer that you did not.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He rises and takes back the clipboard.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>VOSS (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>A dead expedition member interests nobody. An American professor found dead in a ravine, working for an intelligence service your own newspapers would be delighted to name, interests a great many people.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He pats his pockets. Frowns.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>VOSS (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Kuhn. A pencil.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Kuhn produces one. Indy keeps his face as plain as he can.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. THE PEN – CONTINUOUS</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>They&#x27;ve come to the pen. The guards snap to attention. Inside, the specimen sits with its chin on its knees and watches Voss with polite, patient disinterest, like a man at the opera who&#x27;s paid for worse seats.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Voss&#x27;s hand goes to his breast pocket and stays there.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>VOSS</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Do you have children, Dr. Jones?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>No.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>VOSS</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Then you are freer than I am. I have two girls, six and eight. They wanted to know what a yeti is. I said I would find out. I would like to go home and tell them it is a kind of goat.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>It is the least official thing he&#x27;s said. It is also the most frightening, because Indy understands that Voss means every word and that none of it will change a single order he gives.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>VOSS (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Walk away tonight. Take your guide and whatever grief she carries. The thing in that pen becomes a photograph, then a specimen in a case, then a curiosity nobody remembers.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He&#x27;s marking the days on the fence post. Forty-nine when I counted. Fifty-two by now.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>VOSS</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Who is?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Your photograph.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Voss looks at the post. He looks at the creature, which looks back.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>VOSS</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I will have it sanded.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He makes a note with Kuhn&#x27;s pencil.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>VOSS (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Your answer, Dr. Jones?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>No.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Voss nods, as at a figure he had expected.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>VOSS</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Then I regret that our walk has been a waste of good lamp oil. Guards. See Dr. Jones to the perimeter. Gently, for now.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. SHOMO CAMP – DUSK</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>They walk him out through a camp that has begun to smell of onions: two guards and a rifle at his back. Men drift toward the mess tent in twos and threes. At the bell the whole camp will empty into it, as it does every night, and the only ones left outside will be the two at the pen, who have been told the lion is asleep.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>His escort is FELDWEBEL STOLL, a face like a closed door and Indy&#x27;s own belt looped over one shoulder like a bandolier. Three paces behind, the YOUNG GUARD with the scab on his chin carries his rifle like a broom.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Voss gave the order in the passive voice. Nobody could have said whom it was meant for. Stoll, Indy gathers, has decided it was meant for him.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>STOLL</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The Herr Doktor said perimeter.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He said gently, too.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>STOLL</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Yes.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He weighs the sentence.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>STOLL (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>To shoot is not gentle. It is loud.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He points past the mess tent, past the clothesline where three frozen tunics stand stiff in the dusk, to where the camp ends in a cut of broken ground.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>STOLL (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>A man falls. It is very quiet.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That&#x27;s one reading.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>STOLL</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It is the only reading.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He takes hold of him.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. BEHIND THE MESS TENT – CONTINUOUS</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy has been in a fair number of fights and has never won one by being polite. He drives an elbow into Stoll&#x27;s ribs, which is like elbowing a sack of coal. Stoll answers with a fist that puts him through the CLOTHESLINE.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The line goes, and the frozen tunics with it, landing across Indy&#x27;s chest with the weight of fresh lumber. He rolls, comes up with one by the collar, and swings. The sleeves are stiff as oars. It takes Stoll across the side of the head with a sound like a door slammed in a cellar, and the big man sits down in the mud with a look of honest surprise.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>And then gets up again, because he is a Feldwebel.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>YOUNG GUARD</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(raising his rifle, no idea what to do with it)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Please. Please, I do not—</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Stay there.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He ducks a swing that would have taken his ear. Goes round the pole. Catches his own belt where it hangs over Stoll&#x27;s shoulder and pulls. It isn&#x27;t a whip. The whip is in a trunk across the camp, which is the sort of thing Indy intends to take up with the universe later. It&#x27;s a belt with a brass buckle. But Indy has spent twenty years learning what to do with a length of leather, and his wrist knows before he does. The buckle flicks round Stoll&#x27;s forearm and bites. Indy heaves. The Feldwebel leaves his feet, and then, with the enormous stubborn dignity of a man who has never been dragged anywhere, gets back onto them and wraps both arms round Indy&#x27;s chest.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>STOLL</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(almost kindly)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It is quiet.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He begins to walk.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy&#x27;s boots leave the ground. The edge is ten yards off. Eight. He can smell the old blue cold rising out of the cut. He hammers at Stoll&#x27;s ears with the buckle. He might as well hammer at a church.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Across the camp, a single BELL begins to ring.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Stoll takes another step.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>And out of the dark behind the generator shed comes a noise like someone beating a carpet with a coal scuttle.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A MULE, at full gallop. Not graceful. A mule does not gallop so much as conduct a long serious argument with the ground, at speed, and lose none of it. It bursts out of the dusk with its ears flat, and low along its neck, hair loose, gripping a Lee-Enfield by the barrel like an axe handle, rides TSERING.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The mule passes Stoll&#x27;s backside at about eleven miles an hour.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>It is the honest gray, who has bitten everyone who ever stood behind it, and who now finds himself running for the first time in his career directly at a man&#x27;s rear end with full permission.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The bell covers most of what Stoll says. He lets go. Indy hits the ground. Tsering is off the mule before it stops. The rifle butt comes round in a short flat arc. Stoll sits down for the second time that evening and stays there, with a neat double row of tooth marks through the seat of his trousers and an expression of profound betrayal.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The Young Guard has raised both hands without being asked. His lips are moving.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>YOUNG GUARD</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Ich will—</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He tries it in English.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>YOUNG GUARD (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I would like to go home.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I know. Sit down.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He buckles his belt back on.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Tie him. Make it tight.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>YOUNG GUARD</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Tight. Please. They will ask.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She uses the mule&#x27;s lead rope, with five small hard knots exactly like the ones in the red cord on Indy&#x27;s wrist, and sits the boy against the clothesline pole. From the mess tent comes a roar of laughter and the Bavarian voice doing his slow impression: &quot;Kameraden. We are here to serve science.&quot; Nobody comes out to see about a bell, a mule, or a man in the mud.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The boy shuts his eyes and begins to whistle softly through his teeth, like a person who has heard absolutely nothing.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Tsering looks at Indy, at the wrecked clothesline, at three frozen tunics lying in the mud with their arms still raised, and at the bell, which has just stopped.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Seven.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It&#x27;s seven. I was on my way out.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You were on your way over a cliff.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She swings back onto the mule, which stands for it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You are late. Get up.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>On that?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It is the fastest thing I had.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy, who once outran a boulder in Peru, climbs up behind her without another word. They go out through the north perimeter at a bone-rattling trot, into a sky just going dark, to the sound of a mess tent laughing at a man who isn&#x27;t there.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. RIDGE ABOVE SHOMO – NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>They&#x27;re back on the ridge within the hour. Indy tells Pemba what Voss offered and what he refused.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PEMBA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Two days becomes one. I heard it from the cook. Voss wants the specimen crated and the camp broken by tomorrow night.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Then we move tonight. Before they&#x27;re ready for us instead of after. I don&#x27;t need to steal the creature yet. I need Kessler&#x27;s report before it&#x27;s crated onto that plane with Voss&#x27;s signature on the cover. Once it reaches Berlin, there&#x27;s no pulling it back out.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>And Norbu? Does the report matter more than that?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>No. But they&#x27;re the same fight now. Kessler wrote enough in that trunk to hang himself on both counts, if it reaches the right hands instead of Berlin&#x27;s.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JO</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>And the plane? One airplane, forty-one drums of petrol, six men sitting on a dry lake with nothing to do. I could look at that all day.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>We decided not to touch it. Burn it, and Kessler sends for another.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JO</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I&#x27;m not going to burn it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She polishes the Speed Graphic&#x27;s lens on her sleeve.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JO (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I&#x27;m going to take their picture.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>She will be shot.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JO</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I&#x27;ll be popular. Nobody was ever shot who offered to put a man&#x27;s face in LIFE.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Jo—</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JO</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You take your camp. I&#x27;ll take the airfield. Whatever happens in there tonight, nobody leaves.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(a beat)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Don&#x27;t be a hero.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JO</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I&#x27;m a photographer. We stand at the back.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Tsering passes round a tin of lamp soot. They blacken their faces. Indy&#x27;s comes out streaky.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You look like a man who lost a fight with a chimney.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She fixes it with her thumb, hard, the way she tightens a pack strap.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Pemba draws in the dirt with a stick. A line for the north edge, a square for the generator shed, a circle for the pen.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PEMBA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Voss has doubled the guard. Kessler&#x27;s trunk was in his tent. Today I saw it carried out and put by the mule line. Two of Voss&#x27;s men carried it, not like a trunk. Like a coffin.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He looks at Indy.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PEMBA (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I tell you so you will know it may be locked.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It was locked last time.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PEMBA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It was. You opened it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He rubs out the circle with his boot.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PEMBA (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Please do it again, but faster.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I&#x27;ll do my best.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PEMBA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You said that last time also.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>For the first time since Indy met him, Pemba smiles, thinly, and doesn&#x27;t seem to enjoy it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Tsering oils the Lee-Enfield with a rag from her sleeve.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>If I fall, you will not carry me. You will carry the paper.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I&#x27;ll carry both.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You are a poor liar, and a worse mule.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She slides the bolt home and listens to it, the small final sound of a good lock.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I have decided you are not completely useless.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Pemba holds out a twist of paper: dried apricots, black and hard as buttons.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PEMBA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>For afterward. A man who has been afraid should eat something sweet.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Where did you get these?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PEMBA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The quartermaster&#x27;s tent. I have carried his boxes for four years. I decided I was owed.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy eats one. It tastes of sunlight and a very long time ago.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(to Tsering)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That&#x27;s the nicest thing you&#x27;ve said to me.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Do not get used to it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. THE SLOPE ABOVE THE CAMP – AFTER MIDNIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>They go down the way she taught him: low and slow over ground that punishes haste. They&#x27;re in sight of the generator shed when a HAND closes on Pemba&#x27;s shoulder out of the dark.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A voice barks something in German that needs no translation.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy and Tsering go flat. Pemba doesn&#x27;t run. He stands very still as a torch finds his face. Two of Voss&#x27;s men march him toward the lit tents, shoulders set in the posture of a man who has decided how much truth to spend keeping two other people alive.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Tsering comes up onto one knee. Indy catches her round the waist with one arm and clamps the other hand over her mouth. She drives an elbow into his ribs. Again. The second he feels in his teeth. She bites the heel of his hand through the glove and he makes a sound he&#x27;s glad the wind takes.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(barely a breath, against her ear)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Tsering. If you go, he&#x27;s wasted.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She says something into his palm in Tibetan. He doesn&#x27;t need a translator. She keeps saying it until the torch swings away. Then she stops all at once and lies with her breath hissing through her nose.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He takes his hand off her mouth. There&#x27;s blood on it. He isn&#x27;t sure whose.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She doesn&#x27;t apologize. She wipes her chin on her sleeve and looks at the camp.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Move.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. VOSS&#x27;S TENT – NIGHT (INTERCUT)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Pemba, questioned hard enough to leave marks that will take weeks to fade, tells Voss&#x27;s men the Americans plan to strike at dawn, and that the target is the pen. It is a lie with just enough detail to survive a first round of questioning and just enough wrongness to buy two people a window.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. SCREE SLOPE – CONTINUOUS</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A ROCKET TROOPER lands on the scree thirty feet from them and stands up. Indy gets his first look at the man inside the harness.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Very young. The leather helmet has slipped off his forehead. His face is white and wet. He is working the valve on his left cylinder with a shaking thumb, the way a boy works a stuck bicycle chain.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He sees them. He stops.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Tsering has the Lee-Enfield at her shoulder before Indy finishes turning. The boy raises his hands. For two seconds nobody breathes.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Then a voice from the camp shouts &quot;BRANDT!&quot; and the boy&#x27;s face closes. His thumb finds the valve.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She shoots the cylinder, not the man.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The pack goes up with a BANG like a door slammed in a cathedral. Brandt falls on his back in the scree with his hands over his face, alive, swearing quietly in German, the same word over and over, while the dark fills with light.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I did not want to kill him.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I know.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(working the bolt)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I would have. Move.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. THE MULE LINE – NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The one part of the camp nobody thinks could be a target. Under a tarp at the foot of a stack of big boxes, exactly where Pemba said, sits the trunk. Two of Voss&#x27;s men guard it from crates, sharing a cigarette.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy holds up two fingers. Tsering goes around the end of the line with the Lee-Enfield held by the barrel, moving as slowly as she taught him to cycle the bolt, and clips the nearer guard below the ear. The other stands up. Indy, who has picked up a tin lid without quite deciding to, hits him with that.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>It isn&#x27;t dignified. It takes four seconds. The cigarette is still burning when they drag both men behind the crates.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The lock is the same lock. He has it open in twenty seconds, a personal best.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The leather folder lies on top, under the jacket with the runes. And beneath it, wrapped in a pair of Kessler&#x27;s clean socks, his own revolver, and in a second pair, coiled like something asleep, the whip.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He hangs the whip back on his belt. It feels like finding a shoe he&#x27;s been missing for a week. He slides the folder into a canvas satchel one guard was using as a pillow and hangs it across his chest.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Then a SHOT whistles past his ear, close enough to move his hair.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. SHOMO CAMP – CONTINUOUS</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>FLOODLIGHTS blaze across the camp, powered by the shed he&#x27;s been counting on for dark. Men pour from the tents half-dressed. From the separate tent comes the low catching roar he last heard over Biscay.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Two harness troopers go up in a spray of fire and gravel and hang over the pen, turning, their flames throwing the valley into hard orange and black. When their packs cough they drop to the drums by the shed, take fresh charges, and go up again. The sky is never empty.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Down!</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She has him by the collar. They go flat behind the crates as rifle fire stitches the tarp.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy lies with his cheek in the dirt. Across forty yards of lit ground he can see the pen. Everyone in the camp is looking at the sky.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The creature isn&#x27;t.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>It stands in the back corner of the pen, where the iron strap runs across the timber, with both hands on the strap. It isn&#x27;t pulling. It is standing quite calmly with its face turned up into the floodlights, like a man waiting for a train to pass before he crosses.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Two bolts on the strap are bright where the paint has worn. He noticed that three nights ago and filed it, the way you file what you&#x27;ve been trained not to follow.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He doesn&#x27;t need to get out. He needs it dark.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>What?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Fifty-two nights. He&#x27;s been working those bolts a quarter turn at a time, and he&#x27;s been waiting for the lights to go out.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>It comes to him whole, the way a translation does after a day of pushing at it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I can&#x27;t kill the floodlights from here. I need the shed. Which means I need the camp out of the way.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He points at the line of animals along the rope, forty of them, rolling their eyes at the fire overhead.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>How fast can you cut those loose?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Tsering looks at the mules. At the tents.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Then she does something he has never seen her do. She laughs, one hard bark, and draws her knife.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. THE MULE LINE – CONTINUOUS</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She goes down the picket line in one pull. The rope parts at every post. Forty frightened animals discover, in the same second, that they are free and the sky is on fire.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>They go through the camp like water through a broken dam.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The mess tent folds under them. The big tent follows, and from inside comes a thin strangled shout that sounds, absurdly, like a man calling for his records. A cook goes over a drum of kerosene and comes up swearing in Bavarian. Tsering fires the Lee-Enfield into the air; the rear of the herd turns toward the noise, the front toward the lights, and the whole screaming mass sweeps across the open ground with Voss&#x27;s guards diving left and right.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy runs in their lee, the satchel against his chest and a mule&#x27;s flank two feet from his shoulder. When the herd bends toward the pen he cuts left and reaches the shed door.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. GENERATOR SHED – CONTINUOUS</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A MECHANIC stands with a spanner raised and the stunned look of a man who came out to see about a noise. CRACK. The whip takes the spanner out of his hand and sets it on the floor like a retriever. Indy hits him in the stomach, harder than he means to, and steps over him.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The generator is a squat diesel under a tin roof. A black rubber fuel line runs from a drum to the injector.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He cuts it clean through.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Fuel sprays his hands. The engine coughs. Coughs again, a long sick sound. And quits.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Every floodlight in the valley goes out together.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. SHOMO CAMP – CONTINUOUS</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>DARK falls like a lid.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>For a few seconds there is only the roar of the troopers overhead, two moving flames on a black field. And under it, from the pen, a sound Indy hears clearly in the ringing quiet:</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>An iron strap, long loosened, tearing off its timber.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He runs. He makes it eleven steps.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The nearer flame wheels in the sky like a lighthouse beam, sweeps down the shed, and finds him. Indy does the only thing available to a man in a spotlight. He stands perfectly still and hopes to be mistaken for a post.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>FALK (O.S.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Dr. Jones.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The flame comes down thirty feet away, boots first, in a spray of gravel and steam, like a man stepping off a streetcar. LEUTNANT FALK is young, with a pleasant bony face under a leather helmet, a pistol in one hand and a pair of Zeiss field glasses bouncing on his chest.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>FALK</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Forgive me. I have been asked to be firm. Leutnant Falk.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He bows his head a quarter of an inch.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Then he lifts two fingers to the peak of his helmet. Polite. Almost friendly. Indy has seen that salute before, through the glass nose of a Junkers over the Bay of Biscay.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Biscay. You were in the Ju 88.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>FALK</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I was. I am told you pulled very hard on my man&#x27;s strap. He asked me to send his regards.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Tell him he&#x27;s welcome.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>FALK (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It is not personal.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You were on the glacier. Two of your men didn&#x27;t come out. And you whistled.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>FALK</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Yes. For them. It is the only prayer I was taught. Is it a fault?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He says it with real interest, and shoots at him.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy dives behind a stack of gray steel drums stenciled with a skull, giving off a sharp clean smell like the stuff you pour on a wound. The bullet whangs off the nearest. Falk&#x27;s face changes.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>FALK (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Please do not hide behind those. They are extremely expensive.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Then don&#x27;t shoot them.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>FALK</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>No. I must not.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He considers the problem with his head on one side, like a man looking for a better parking space. The valves hiss and he rises, slowly. Ten feet. Twelve. For an angle over the drums.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>It is exactly what Indy has been waiting for.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He comes out at a dead run, jumps, and catches both boots.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The pack SCREAMS. For a moment it can&#x27;t decide whether to lift the extra hundred and seventy pounds or throw them off. Falk says something in German that is probably a prayer.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Then the flames steady into a long roar, and the ground falls away.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. SHOMO CAMP – IN THE AIR – CONTINUOUS</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Twenty feet. Forty. Indy hangs from a stranger&#x27;s ankles with the satchel swinging against his ribs and the camp turning below him like a map he drew himself. The black shed. The wrecked tents. The mules still stampeding in circles. Torches bobbing like fireflies. At the corner of the pen, a small patient hump that does not look up.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>FALK</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Herr Doktor, please let go. It will be easier for both of us.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You first.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>FALK</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I am unable.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The pistol comes down past his hip, hunting for an angle. Indy goes hand over hand up the harness until his face is an inch from the pack. Two cylinders. A cross-valve where the tubing meets. And a gauge needle already sinking. Falk came down for fuel, not a fight, and has perhaps half a charge left. With a hundred and seventy pounds of archaeologist hanging off him, a lot less.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He starts to count.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Falk gets the pistol round and jams it against Indy&#x27;s ribs. Indy catches the wrist with his left hand, finds the cross-valve by feel, a stiff brass wheel the size of a biscuit, and turns it as hard as he can.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The left cylinder shuts off. The right goes on burning. The whole rig, which had been rising like an elevator, lurches into a long lopsided SPIN.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>FALK (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Please! Please do not do that!</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I read it in a bulletin!</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The pistol goes off into the sky.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>They corkscrew over the camp in a great shrieking spiral, trailing flame, past the tent tops, past the shed&#x27;s chimney, so low a startled Bavarian looks up from the mud and falls over backward.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>At twenty-six the pack COUGHS. Falk drops six feet. Catches himself. Drops six more.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Thirty-one. Thirty-two.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>They come down through the ruins of the mess tent like a bomb through a circus. Canvas gives. A table folds. A tureen of cold soup takes the whole impact with a sound like the end of the world. The pack gives one last sorrowful cough and goes out.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The two of them lie in a heap of ropes, playing cards and onions, steam rising off the leather.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy rolls off, gasping, trying to remember which arm he uses for things. Falk lies with his helmet askew and his face white as a plate, still holding the pistol. Slowly, with the exquisite manners of a man who hasn&#x27;t noticed he&#x27;s concussed, he begins to raise it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>FALK</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I am so sorry, Herr Doktor.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A shadow falls across him: stout, aproned, a pipe clamped in its teeth, holding a cast-iron skillet. The MESS COOK looks down at the young lieutenant with the infinite weariness of a man who has put up with the army for three years.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He brings the skillet down on the helmet with a noise like a church bell.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Falk sighs and goes to sleep.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The cook looks at the skillet. He looks at Indy. He takes the pipe from his mouth, says one word in German that Indy doesn&#x27;t know and understands perfectly, and puts the pipe back.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>COOK</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Quitt.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(hoarse)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Quits. Thank you for the bread.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The cook shrugs, as if bread were nothing, and goes off to see what has become of his soup.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Overhead the second flame is swinging round, searching. Indy checks the satchel, still buckled, still heavy. He pulls himself out of the wreckage and runs for the ledge with the taste of onions in his mouth and, for the first time since Biscay, a feeling uncomfortably close to joy.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. THE PEN – CREVASSE EDGE – NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The only way out nobody guards is along the pen&#x27;s far side, where the ground falls into a crevasse. Tsering said she&#x27;d meet him there. She isn&#x27;t there yet.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The pen is thirty feet to his left, with a gap in its lower corner where the strap used to be.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The creature comes out through the gap at a walk.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>It is larger than it was in the pen. It comes over the broken shoring on all fours and straightens, and in the orange light from the sky it is not a monster and not a man.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>It carries something in its right hand. Indy doesn&#x27;t see what until it stops six feet away and looks down at him from a height that makes him feel like a student.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A tin cup. The rice pressed down inside with a thumb.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Neither of them moves.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy has a revolver with five rounds and has never been less sure he wants it. After a moment he does the only thing he can think of. He lifts his left hand, slowly, palm out, and the red cord Tsering tied on his wrist swings forward in the light.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The creature looks at the cord. Looks at him.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>It makes a sound deep in its chest, very low, with no words in it but with a shape: the shape of a thing said once to someone who might, in time, understand.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Then a trooper sweeps overhead, low, his pack howling, and the flame lights the ledge white. The creature flinches. It goes past Indy in a single bound, the shoulder catching him and spinning him round.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>And the ledge, which has been holding for years on nothing but frost, gives way under his boots.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He catches the lip with both hands. His body swings out over the dark. The satchel strap slides off his shoulder and down his arm. He feels it go. Feels the weight leave him. Hears it hit something below and make no sound.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Tsering&#x27;s hands close on his collar from above.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I have you. Do not let go of me.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He doesn&#x27;t.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>When she has hauled him onto the snow and he lies on his back, fuel still wet on his hands and the generator silent and the camp screaming in the dark, he looks up at the sky and understands, with the clarity that comes only at the worst possible moment, that the report is gone.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>And that there is exactly one way left to get it back.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. THE CREVASSE – NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Narrower than the one Indy imagined, which is the only mercy the mountain offers tonight.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He goes over the edge on a rope Tsering has anchored round a boulder with hands that stop shaking the moment there is work to do. The ice closes around him in grades of dark: blue-gray near the surface, black within thirty feet, with the cold of glacier water that kills a strong swimmer faster than a weak one, because the strong ones keep trying long after the sense has gone out of trying.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He finds the satchel by touch, wedged in a crack six feet to his left, out of reach of any arm. He unhooks the whip. The first throw misses. The second finds the strap. The third brings the satchel swinging into his hand like a trout, the canvas already stiff.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. CREVASSE EDGE – CONTINUOUS</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Tsering hauls him up hand over hand. He can&#x27;t feel his fingers well enough to know he still holds it until he sees the satchel clutched against his chest.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Breathe. Breathe, American, before you forget how.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She pulls him flat onto the snow.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Above them, the fighting has moved up the slope toward the snowfield, where Voss&#x27;s men are chasing a shape that moves faster across broken ground than any of them can follow.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>But one man hasn&#x27;t gone. KESSLER stands at the crevasse&#x27;s edge, pistol hanging loose in one hand, his face lit from below by a dropped torch guttering in the snow.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(getting to his knees)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You didn&#x27;t fire.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KESSLER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It went past me. Within the length of this arm. It had a cup in its hand, did you see? I had the pistol up. I have shot a great many things, Dr. Jones, and I did not decide to lower it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He turns the pistol over as if it might carry an inscription.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KESSLER (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It looked at me and knew precisely what I was. A man is not often given an accurate review.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Tsering is on her feet with her father&#x27;s Lee-Enfield steady on Kessler&#x27;s chest. Indy hadn&#x27;t expected the steadiness. He should have.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Norbu.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Kessler does not flinch at the name.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KESSLER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He pronounced the German place names better than my own men. I corrected his English once and he corrected my Tibetan, and he was right.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He looks at the torch.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KESSLER (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I gave the order. I told Lang and Brenner to stop him before the lower valley. I did not say how. They understood me perfectly, and I knew they would.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>His voice stays level, which makes it worse.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KESSLER (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I have spent four years improving the verb.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Her finger moves on the trigger, a fraction. Indy understands that the next half second belongs entirely to her, and that anything he does will either save Kessler&#x27;s life or cost Tsering something she will carry the rest of hers.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Tsering.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Do not tell me what this costs me.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I&#x27;m telling you what happens next. If you kill him here, nobody in Berlin or Lhasa or London ever hears the name Norbu. If he writes it down, they do.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The torch gutters lower. She holds the gun steady, longer than the cold or the hour should have allowed. Then she lowers it. It isn&#x27;t graceful. Her arm has simply run out of whatever was holding it up.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You write down every word you said to me. And you sign it. And I take it out of this country myself.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She doesn&#x27;t lower her eyes.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>If I ever hear your name in any paper, I will finish tonight.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Kessler looks at her. Then he nods once, sits down heavily in the snow, and begins, with hands that Indy notes are shaking for the first time, to write.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A flashbulb goes off like a small sun.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JO (O.S.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That&#x27;s a lovely confession. Take your time. I&#x27;m still changing film.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Kessler flinches. Tsering spins with the rifle up.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Jo Calder picks her way down the slope out of the blue afterglow, Speed Graphic slung across her chest like a bazooka, Colt .45 in her free hand, out of breath, and smelling powerfully of petrol. She looks at the rifle pointed at her.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JO</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Honey, if you&#x27;re going to point that at somebody, point it at him.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Tsering lowers the rifle. A notch.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You&#x27;re late.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JO</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I had a lot of drums.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She sniffs her sleeve, winces.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JO (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The plane&#x27;s done. Six boys, one lake, and not a drop of the good stuff left in Asia.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Any trouble?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JO</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Their idea of a guard was a card game. I said &quot;LIFE magazine,&quot; and they fought over who got to stand in front.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She shrugs.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JO (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Men are easy.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(to Indy, flat)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I like her.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You said she was loud.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I like loud things from a distance.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Jo grins. Then she looks down at the pen moving in Kessler&#x27;s hand.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JO</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Is that what I think it is?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It is my brother&#x27;s name. And what was done to him.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The wisecracking goes out of Jo&#x27;s face. What&#x27;s left is harder and more useful.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JO</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Then I&#x27;ll be your witness, ma&#x27;am. Every page, a negative of each, in three different places by sunrise. The negatives are yours. Nobody buries anything on my watch.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She takes the pistol out of Kessler&#x27;s limp hand, as one lifts a salt shaker off a neighbor&#x27;s plate.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JO (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I&#x27;ll hold this.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Then the shouting changes above them. All four look up.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. THE SNOWFIELD – NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Two hundred yards up the slope, a pale bowl in the dark, lit by the last trooper hovering over it on a fresh charge. By his long orange flicker Indy sees everything at once.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Voss&#x27;s men, fanned across the slope, rifles up. A machine-gun team wrestling an MG 34 onto its bipod. Four more dragging a net the size of a tennis court.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>And in the middle, quite alone, standing in the open like a man who has come out to see what all the noise was about: the CREATURE. Without the fence to measure against, he keeps getting bigger. He still holds the tin cup.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>VOSS (O.S.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(megaphone, shrill)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Lebendig! The specimen is to be recovered alive! Unharmed!</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KESSLER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(murmuring)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Voss. He wants a photograph.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Jo&#x27;s hands have gone to the Speed Graphic before her brain catches up.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JO</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Oh, that&#x27;s a cover. That&#x27;s every cover for ten years.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She brings it up. Then the creature turns his head and looks at them, down two hundred yards of night. She stops. She lowers the camera.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JO (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(to nobody)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>No. Not that one.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The machine gun opens up: a long ripping burst fired high to herd him. The creature looks at it the way a cat looks at a vacuum cleaner.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The net goes spinning out and comes down over him. The whole line of soldiers begin, absurdly, to cheer.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He closes one hand on the netting and pulls.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Four men who&#x27;ve been holding the ropes leave the ground in a neat row, like laundry, and sail off across the snow in four directions. The net comes with him. He shakes it out once, the way a man shakes a tablecloth, and flings it back over the machine-gun nest. The gun stops. So do the gunners, in a heap, under a hundred pounds of wet hemp.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JO</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(faintly)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Nobody told me he was going to be this good.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Every rifle on the slope goes off together. They&#x27;re aimed low, for Voss&#x27;s sake, and the creature moves like water round a stone: a stride left, and the bullets eat the snow where he was. One enormous arm sweeps sideways and takes three men off their feet like a scythe through wheat. He comes up with the machine gun in both fists, tripod and all, and uses it. It rings off helmets like a church bell. A sergeant goes over backward. Another sits down quite politely, holding his jaw.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The creature finds the weapon unsatisfactory. He bends it double across his knee and drops it. The sound it makes hitting the snow is the saddest noise Indy has ever heard from a machine.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>VOSS (O.S.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Forward! Alive! The specimen is to be—</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A pause.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>VOSS (O.S.) (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Recovered!</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JO</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(delighted)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He dropped the &quot;alive.&quot;</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The survivors come in all at once, a dozen at least, bayonets out. The creature lets them. He stands in the middle of the snowfield and looks down at the white under his feet. Tilts his head. Listens.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(her hand on Indy&#x27;s arm)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Oh. He knows the snow.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He draws back one huge fist and drives it down into the shelf like a man striking an anvil.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The crack runs away from his hand in every direction, a bright thread across the bowl. The whole slope below him sits up and sighs. The first line of soldiers has time to look down. The second has time to shout.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Then half an acre of snow, with twelve men standing on it, folds and slides with a roar down the long white slope toward the broken fence, taking a net, a tripod, a good deal of Voss&#x27;s dignity, and every rifle on the field.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Everyone in this valley knows the snow, except the Germans.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That is why we are still alive.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. CREVASSE EDGE – CONTINUOUS</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The last trooper has been dropping back to the drums for fuel and climbing out again, circling, waiting for a clean pass. He comes down now. He doesn&#x27;t go for the creature. He goes for the knot of figures at the edge of the crevasse: the satchel, the man with a pen.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Whatever Voss&#x27;s orders were, the young man has a better one of his own. The harness howls. The flame lengthens to a white lance, and he comes down the slope at forty miles an hour with a pistol thrust ahead like a man spearing a fish.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Tsering begins to count under her breath, in Tibetan.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Thirty.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Forty.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Jo, if you&#x27;ve got anything—</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JO</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Hold your horses.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She has the lens cap off, a fresh bulb snapped into the reflector, and her thumb on the shutter. She has, Indy notices, the steady stillness of a woman who has stood in front of worse than this and made it smile.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The trooper fills the sky. Indy can see his teeth.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JO (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(gently)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Look at me, sweetheart.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She fires.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The bulb goes off like the end of the world. It fills the crevasse with white. The trooper, who has spent the last hour in the dark with his eyes wide open, SCREAMS and flings both hands over his face, and the flame, so perfectly aimed, goes howling off in a great blind curve. He misses Kessler by a yard and the crevasse by another. Indy feels the heat pass over his scalp like an iron.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He is already coming round again: blind, steering by the howl of his own flame, straight back at the crevasse. Lower this time.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy has the whip off his belt. He drops to one knee at the boulder where Tsering&#x27;s rope is anchored, threads the whip&#x27;s handle through the loop of the knot, and hauls it tight with his teeth.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>What are you doing?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Giving him something to be on the end of.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He stands up in the snow, alone, directly in the trooper&#x27;s path, with a coiled bullwhip and the perfect stillness of a man hoping to be mistaken for a post.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The flame comes down the slope like a lit fuse. Indy can see the scorched face behind it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>CRACK.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The lash uncoils across the dark and wraps the trooper&#x27;s ankle.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The line goes taut. The knot holds. The boulder groans. The trooper stops in mid-air as though he has run out of sky. Then he swings, a rocket-powered pendulum, round and round the boulder on fifteen feet of leather, screaming, his flame carving a white circle in the snow. Indy rides the other end of it on his heels, boots plowing two furrows, and does not let go of the knot.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KESSLER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(not looking up from his page)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That is a remarkably good whip.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(through his teeth)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It&#x27;s an old whip.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Seventy.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The pack SPITS. Sputters.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Out on the slope, a great shadow comes up off the snow with a boulder the size of a bread oven in both hands. The creature, who has apparently been waiting only for somebody to hold him still, leans back.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>And throws.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>It takes the trooper out of the sky in a single hit. The flame goes out. The whip goes slack in Indy&#x27;s hands. The man drops with a thin rising shriek into the runout and lands with a soft thump, his ankle still in the lash, and does not move, and does not die: the pack has folded around him like a cradle. Somewhere in the dark Voss&#x27;s megaphone gives a small squawk and says nothing further.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>It is very quiet.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The creature stands alone on the scoured slope, steam rising off his shoulders. A few of the men he&#x27;s scattered crawl toward the camp. He doesn&#x27;t follow them. He lowers his arms.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Then, slowly, he looks down the long slope toward the crevasse, and Indy feels the weight of that gaze come across two hundred yards of darkness and stop, like a hand laid flat, on the four of them at the edge.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Kessler has stopped writing. Nobody breathes.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The creature raises the tin cup, once, until it catches a little of the dying light. It might be a salute. It might be a man checking that his dishes are clean.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Then he turns and goes up the snowfield and over the ridge, into the high white country where no airplane will ever follow. The dark closes behind him.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(softly, to the place where he was)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Migoi.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KESSLER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Accurate.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He goes on writing.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Jo stands with the Speed Graphic hanging in her hands and a very peculiar look on her face. She has a great many things to say, Indy can tell, and she is going to say none of them. She takes a flask from her jacket, drinks, and holds it out to him.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JO</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I had the shot. I had the whole shot.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I know.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JO</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Best picture I ever didn&#x27;t take.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She screws the cap back on.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JO (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Don&#x27;t tell Luce.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. CREVASSE EDGE – LATER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Kessler writes until the torch burns down to a stub. When he has signed the last page he holds it out to Tsering without looking at her. She takes it without looking at him. That is the whole of the exchange.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy opens the satchel on his knee by the last of the light and takes out the leather folder. For a minute he only holds it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>It is the best thing he has ever put his hands on, and he knows it. Tables. Drawings. A careful account, in a scholar&#x27;s hand, of what the thing in the pen is, how it stands, how it eats, what it counts. No museum in the world wouldn&#x27;t hang his name over the door for it. Somewhere at the back of his head, in the lecture hall where he lives, a voice a good deal like his own has already begun drafting the footnotes.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KESSLER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You will want to publish. I did. It is a very good report.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It&#x27;s the best I&#x27;ve ever read.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>That is the trouble with it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He takes out the photographs of the Tibetan men first: the faces on the stool with calipers at their temples. Squares them into a stack. Holds them out to Tsering.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Those go back to their families. Whoever can be found.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She takes them in both hands and does not thank him. He understands this is a courtesy.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Then he tears from his notebook the pages where he copied Kessler&#x27;s lines and lays them on top of the folder.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You kept it dry.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I&#x27;m about to un-keep it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JO</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(very softly, for her)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Indy. That&#x27;s the story of the decade.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The mountain made this call once already, on the ice. I&#x27;m just following instructions. It&#x27;s a skull in a case the minute anyone but him reads it. Somebody in Berlin or Washington or London reads this and starts planning a better-equipped expedition. I&#x27;d like the mountain to stay a rumor a while longer.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He picks Kessler&#x27;s dropped torch out of the snow and puts it to the corner of the folder. The paper tries to curl away, the way paper does. Then gives up. It burns slowly, with a great deal of light. The four of them watch four years of one man&#x27;s work go up in a column of sparks that the wind carries east.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KESSLER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(hoarse, tired)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It is Old Babylonian.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>What?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KESSLER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The sign. In your footnote.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy looks at the fire.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I know. I&#x27;ve known since the day it ran. Write that down too, if you like.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Kessler lets out a single dry breath that might, in a happier man, have been a laugh.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Tsering watches until the last page goes.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Good. It weighed a great deal.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. EDGE OF THE CAMP – DAWN</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Gray light. Smoke. Wrecked tents. Pemba limps toward them out of the confusion: bruised, battered, but upright, having slipped away in the chaos of the specimen&#x27;s escape.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Tsering reaches him first. She says nothing. She puts a hand against his bruised face and holds it there a moment. He nods, embarrassed.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Of the creature there is no trace in the camp. But above it, on the snowfield, Indy finds the tracks: too large, too widely spaced for any animal he can name, heading up and east into country no transport plane will ever reach.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>At the top, where the tracks cross the ridge, he finds the TIN CUP.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>It lies on its side in the middle of the tracks, empty and clean, as if rinsed. Someone has scratched a mark into the rim with a stone.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A single short stroke. The first line of a new row.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He doesn&#x27;t pick it up. Tsering comes up beside him, arms folded, and looks at it for a long time.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(quietly)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>One.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. SNOWFIELD ABOVE SHOMO – NOON</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Voss comes up the snowfield a little before noon, on foot, with what&#x27;s left of his men: eleven, bandaged and filthy and silent, no megaphone. Stoll is among them, a rag tied round his head, his rifle carried like a mop. So is the Young Guard with the scab on his chin, who looks at Indy and then very carefully at the sky.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Voss himself has lost a lens from his spectacles. In his left hand he carries what remains of a gramophone record, a curved black shard, the way a man carries the remains of a hat.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>They stop at thirty yards. Rifles come up in a ragged line, without conviction.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy, Tsering, Jo, Pemba and Kessler stand at the ridge. Kessler has the signed pages folded inside his coat.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>VOSS</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(to nobody in particular)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The documents are to be returned. And the Obersturmbannführer.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The folder&#x27;s ash. The confession isn&#x27;t yours. Miss Calder has every page on film, Herr Doktor, in three places, and you won&#x27;t find all three.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He takes a step forward.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You wanted a report so clean no journalist could prove it a fraud. You&#x27;re holding the dirtiest paper in Asia: a Reich officer, signed and dated, confessing murder on Tibetan soil. It goes to LIFE magazine, and it is going to be very hard to turn into a curiosity.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Voss looks at Kessler.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KESSLER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It is true. I signed it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>VOSS</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Feldwebel.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He lifts his hand.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Stoll does not raise his rifle. He looks at the Herr Doktor with his closed-door face.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>STOLL</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(English like a dropped crate)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The Herr Doktor said gently. To shoot is loud.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(quietly)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Do not speak. It is noon.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The WIND STOPS.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>It doesn&#x27;t die. It ceases, all at once, and the silence is so total Indy can hear a rifle sling creak thirty yards away. A man coughs, and is ashamed.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>And from somewhere far above them comes a sound: low, a long note with no words in it and the shape of a word, like a horn blown a mile off under water.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Then it is answered from the left, from farther up. Then from the right. Not one voice but many, passing the call from ridge to ridge the way fires are passed.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Eleven rifles go down by degrees, without anyone telling them to.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. THE HIGH RIM – CONTINUOUS</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>On the rim across the saddle, in the flat white noon, with their hair hanging straight because there is no wind to lift it, stand the PEOPLE OF THE MOUNTAIN.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy tries to count them and his eyes give up at thirty. They are tall, and still, and spaced along the skyline like a congregation. They look down at the men on the ridge without hostility and without any particular interest, as people look at weather.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Jo has the Speed Graphic halfway to her face. She looks at Tsering. Tsering does not say a word.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Jo lowers the camera.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>That is the second best picture she never took.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>On the rim, the nearest figure lifts a fist the size of a haybale and brings it down on a boulder. Four short blows. Then one long, flat, heavy.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Another answers from farther along. Then another. Then all of them, down the whole skyline, until the noon is full of one sound: four short, one long. Four short, one long. The bell Indy rang on the ice, made by a hundred hands.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(barely a breath)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Four short.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>One long.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The fifth day.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>VOSS</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(very quietly)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>A kind of goat.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Nobody answers him.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Then the wind comes back out of the east with a long dry rush, and the rim is empty, as though it had never been anything but snow and sky.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Voss stands a long moment. He looks at the shard in his hand. He opens his fingers and lets it fall.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(to Voss, the first thing she has ever said to him)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Herr Doktor.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Voss turns.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Four short and one long. In this valley it is a count of days. You did not knock, so you were never counted. They are counting you now. That was the fifth day.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Voss takes this in with the stillness of a man reading a final notice.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>VOSS (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Then the aircraft will leave at two. The Obersturmbannführer will accompany it, to account for the loss of the specimen. There is nothing else to account for.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JO</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Will it?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Voss turns. Jo is examining her fingernails.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JO (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It&#x27;s a lovely aircraft. I got to know it very well last night. Forty-one drums, Herr Doktor, and six gentlemen who&#x27;d never been photographed by a lady. It&#x27;s a very nice aircraft. It just isn&#x27;t going anywhere.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Voss closes his eyes.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>VOSS</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Petrol has been... lost.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JO</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Poured. By me. On purpose. Active voice, Herr Doktor.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Somewhere in the ranks a man begins to laugh and is elbowed by the man beside him.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>VOSS</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(very quietly)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Then we shall walk.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JO</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Three hundred miles to the railhead. At sixteen thousand feet.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He turns to Indy. For a moment he is only a tired man in a cracked pair of spectacles.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>VOSS (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I have two daughters. I will tell them it was a very large kind of goat.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Tell them it was shy.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Voss nods, as at a figure he had expected, and leads his men down the snowfield.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Kessler gets up, brushes the snow from his knees, and follows. At the edge of the ridge he turns and says to Tsering:</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KESSLER</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Norbu Wangmo.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He says it correctly.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She says nothing. He goes.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. THE DRY LAKE BED – TWO O&#x27;CLOCK</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The Junkers sits where it always sat, nose to the wind, every door open, a handsome object. Around it the gravel has gone dark and shining, as if the lake had come back for a day: forty-one drums lie on their sides in a long wet stain, and the air smells like a garage on fire. On the wing, in a row, six Luftwaffe ground crew sit with their hands in their laps, each wearing a paper hat folded from a page of LIFE. Across the fuselage, in lipstick, in letters two feet high: THANKS FOR POSING! — J.C.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Voss&#x27;s column files past it without turning its head. At the end of the lake bed Voss stops. Takes off his one-lensed spectacles. Polishes them. Puts them back. And walks on, east, into three hundred miles of nothing. Eleven men follow. Kessler walks at the back, alone, and does not look at the aircraft either.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Behind them, on the wing, one of the airmen lifts a hand and waves. It is the only wave he gets.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>From the saddle high above, Indy, Tsering, Jo and Pemba watch the column dwindle to a line of ants.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. THE TRAIL TO RATENG – NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A small fire. The walk back takes five days instead of four, nobody in a hurry to outrun anything.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Tsering has gone to check the animals. Pemba comes to Indy on his heels, looks at his own hands.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PEMBA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You promised the first thing.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I did.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PEMBA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Please write it now. I would like to see it written.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy takes out the small notebook he&#x27;s carried since Mexico, wet at one corner, and a pencil with a Reich eagle stamped on it. He&#x27;s stopped feeling bad about that.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Tell me how it&#x27;s spelled.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PEMBA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Lhakpa.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He says it slowly.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>PEMBA (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>There is a breath after the first sound, like a man blowing on cold hands. The Germans say it flat, &quot;Lakpa,&quot; and it is wrong. Lhakpa Dorje. He was thirty-four. He had two children, a boy and a girl. The girl is Dechen.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy writes it three times on three lines until Pemba nods. Then adds the date, the place, and the number on the truck&#x27;s door, and signs it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Pemba puts the notebook against his forehead, briefly, the way a man does with a prayer book. And gives it back without a word.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. THE TRAIL – ANOTHER NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Jo walks out with them, which she describes as a favor and Tsering describes, to nobody in particular, as one more animal that talks. She has shot every page of the confession twice by torchlight and sewn the negatives, which are Tsering&#x27;s, into three places: the lining of her jacket, the heel of her left boot, and, over Tsering&#x27;s loud objection, the mule&#x27;s saddle blanket.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The gray mule bites her twice on principle. Then, for reasons of its own, stops.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Her eyes go to the red cord on Indy&#x27;s wrist and stay there.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JO (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Who tied the knots on that?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It&#x27;s wool. It&#x27;s warm.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JO</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Sure it is.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She looks across the fire at Tsering, who studies the flames with the closed, careful face of a woman who has heard nothing. Jo grins like a cat in a creamery.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JO (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Sure it is.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. THE HIGH PASS – DAY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The cairn, hung with white prayer flags that rattle like paper tearing. Tsering stops, as she stopped coming up. Indy waits.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She circles the cairn, touching a stone. On the way in she walked it clockwise. This time she goes the other way, slowly, and he understands without being told that there is a version of the ritual for the living and another for the dead, and that she has just moved from one to the other.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She takes a pinch of tsampa from her pouch and sets it on the cairn, the way you leave a plate for a man who is late.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Go on. I will catch you.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He goes on. She does, after a mile. Her eyes are dry and her face is the same as it was. He doesn&#x27;t say a word about any of it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>It is, he thinks, the one promise he&#x27;s ever managed to keep without being asked.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. THE TRAIL – LAST NIGHT BEFORE RATENG</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A fire. The lights of the trading post visible far below. They read Kessler&#x27;s confession properly by firelight. It is everything Indy hoped: the snares, the order, the four years of silence, signed in a hand that would satisfy any magistrate in any country he can think to send it to.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Under the signature, Kessler has added a line in a smaller, steadier hand. Indy reads it out. Tsering makes him read it twice.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KESSLER (V.O.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I request that the name of the guide be given in all copies as Norbu Wangmo, and not &quot;the guide,&quot; nor &quot;N.&quot; as it appears in my notes. This was an omission of mine and I would correct it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It is a very small thing to ask for. It is also the first time he has used his name.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Folded inside is a photograph, curled at the edges: a broad-faced man on a stool, calipers at his temples. In pencil beneath: N. Wangmo, guide. Spricht Englisch. Kessler has added a second line in ink, small and slanting. Indy translates it aloud because Tsering asks.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>KESSLER (V.O.)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Taken three days before. He asked me not to use it. I have kept it anyway.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She holds the photograph by the edges, as if it might smudge.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He chipped his tooth. On a mule, when he was ten. I told our father it was the mule&#x27;s fault.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She turns it over, looks at the blank back, turns it face up again.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He was thirty-one in this picture. He does not look afraid.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>No.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He looks like he is trying not to laugh at the man with the calipers.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She puts it inside her coat, against her chest, and says nothing else until morning.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. THE RESIDENCY – GANGTOK – DAY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Whitcombe receives them with considerably less surprise than Indy expected from a man who sent him on foot toward a valley full of rocket troopers.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>WHITCOMBE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Shaw&#x27;s cable says there&#x27;s a plane for you at Dinjan on Thursday. Back into the war proper, I&#x27;m afraid. He didn&#x27;t say which theater.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He never does.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>WHITCOMBE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The American mission reached Gangtok on Tuesday, by the way. They&#x27;ve been asking where their academic adviser has got to.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Tell them he&#x27;s late with his grades.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>WHITCOMBE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I said something very like that. They didn&#x27;t laugh either.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Boots on gravel. American voices in the hall, one of them asking loudly whether the professor has been located. Whitcombe, without a flicker, points at the window curtain. Indy is behind it, in a yak-hair coat that could not look less like a professor, when two men in pressed suits walk in and ask whether Dr. Jones has turned up.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>WHITCOMBE (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Not for some days. He did mention he was late with his grades.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>AMERICAN DIPLOMAT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Of course he is.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>They leave. Indy comes out from behind the curtain in a cloud of dust. In the doorway, a flashbulb pops.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JO</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>For the file.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Whitcombe glances at Tsering, who stands at the window with her pack still on her shoulder, having refused every offer of a chair, and at the other young woman leaning in the doorway with two cameras round her neck and a look of patient suffering.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>WHITCOMBE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>And the young ladies?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Miss Wangmo goes her own way from here. She has a report to deliver that doesn&#x27;t belong to either of our governments.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>WHITCOMBE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Good luck to her, then.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He raises his glass half an inch, which is as close to a toast as the man is built to manage.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JO</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>And Miss Calder is going to Washington with a hundred and forty exposures of scenery and a very low opinion of the Himalaya. Also, Mr. Whitcombe, you owe me a drink.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Whitcombe looks at the decanter. Then, with the air of a man amending a treaty, pours a second glass.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy tears out the three lines Pemba approved and lays the page on the desk.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That goes to Shaw with my report. Marked for the file, not for the clerks.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Whitcombe reads it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INSERT – THE PAGE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>In Indy&#x27;s handwriting: Lhakpa Dorje, 34. Killed 14 September, German supply truck, depot road below Shomo. Wife and two children survive. No compensation paid. Below it, three words: Spell it right.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>WHITCOMBE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I&#x27;ll see it&#x27;s done properly.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I didn&#x27;t tell you how.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>WHITCOMBE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>No. But you&#x27;ve underlined it twice, Dr. Jones, and I have been in this country a long time. I know what it means when a man underlines a name twice.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He taps the page.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>WHITCOMBE (CONT&#x27;D)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>London can&#x27;t complain to Lhasa without telling Berlin which of its codes we read. But a Tibetan widow with a husband&#x27;s name, a date, and the number on a German truck door? The Kashag will move mountains for that. They adore a grievance. By the end of the month four hundred very offended Tibetan soldiers will escort the German mission to the border, and nobody will have mentioned a wireless set.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(slowly)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That&#x27;s why it had to be spelled right.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>WHITCOMBE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>A man dies once. His name has to survive the paperwork.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. THE RESIDENCY – GATE – DAY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Tsering leaves before the jeep comes. No ceremony. A nod at the door and the economy of movement he&#x27;s come to know as hers.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>At the gate she stops and turns. From her pack she takes a battered black felt hat, its brim bent by someone else&#x27;s years, and sets it on Indy&#x27;s head without asking. She tilts the brim down with two fingers.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Norbu had a very large head.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>What do I owe you?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>TSERING</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Nothing. It was not mine to sell.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy watches her go down the gravel drive toward the road east, toward wherever four years of carrying a letter ends and whatever comes after begins. He doesn&#x27;t call after her. She hasn&#x27;t asked him to.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Jo goes after her with a small flat tin. At the gate the two of them stand a moment, the tall one and the short one. Jo puts the tin in Tsering&#x27;s palm and closes her fingers over it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Then Tsering takes a length of red wool from her pack and ties it round Jo&#x27;s wrist without asking, with two small hard knots, and pulls each one until it bites.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Jo comes back up the drive with the cord on her wrist and an expression she plainly intends never to explain.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>At the jeep, Whitcombe produces a parcel in brown paper, tied with string, stamped by three customs houses and a monsoon.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>WHITCOMBE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Came up in the diplomatic bag. Addressed to &quot;the gentleman with the whip.&quot;</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy tears the paper. A brass OWL, dented, one wing chipped. A bill is pinned to its chest: To one floorboard, rotten, replaced: £2 6s. To one tailor, startled: no charge. In the margin, in Shaw&#x27;s tidy hand: He says you&#x27;re not to come back until you&#x27;ve paid. — W.S.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It&#x27;s a bill. For an owl.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>WHITCOMBE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>For a floorboard. The owl is a courtesy.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy sets the owl on the jeep&#x27;s dashboard. As the engine turns over it begins, very slowly, to walk toward the edge. Without looking, he reaches out and sets it back.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Then Whitcombe reaches into his coat and gives Indy a small dented tin as if handing over a dispatch.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>WHITCOMBE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Cheese. It&#x27;s dreadful. Take it anyway.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I broke a tooth on the last cheese.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>WHITCOMBE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>This isn&#x27;t that cheese. It&#x27;s from my mother. She sends one every Christmas, and I&#x27;ve never once been able to eat it. I thought you might have better luck.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. C-47 – OVER THE HUMP – DAY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy eats half the cheese. Whitcombe was right: it&#x27;s dreadful. Jo, in the next seat, takes the rest out of his hand without asking and finishes it anyway.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Somewhere below, the last of the high peaks fall away into cloud. Indy sits with the torn notebook in his jacket, thinner by eleven pages. For the first time in his working life he has nothing to publish. Ahead of him the war goes on exactly as it did before he went up.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JO</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(not opening her eyes)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>What was that peak called? The one behind the valley.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Lhari.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JO</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>What does it mean?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy looks out at the cloud where it was.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>INDY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It means the people who named it knew who lived there.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JO</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Wake me when it&#x27;s interesting.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She pulls her cap over her face and puts her boots up on a crate of mail.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Indy turns his wrist in the light. The red cord is still there. He hasn&#x27;t been able to cut it off, and he finds he has stopped trying.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Norbu&#x27;s hat rides on his knee. He puts it on. It fits exactly, which is its own small mystery.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Through the window, far behind them, above the clouds, one white peak catches the sun.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Transition"><Text>FADE OUT.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Transition"><Text>THE END</Text></Paragraph>
</Content>
</FinalDraft>